{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_153","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'date -Is && tail -6 formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-w20261120r11s0final/checks/reg_ch0/logfile.txt && cat implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"2026-11-20T14:55:50+01:00\nSBY 14:46:42 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:02  Skipping step 18..\nSBY 14:46:42 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:02  Skipping step 19..\nSBY 14:46:42 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:02  Checking assumptions in step 20..\nSBY 14:46:45 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:05  Checking assertions in step 20..\nSBY 14:47:45 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:05  waiting for solver (1 minute)\nSBY 14:51:45 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:05:05  waiting for solver (5 minutes)\n# Bench EX recovery\n\nEX compares both candidate next PCs against the oldest unissued full PC. IF registers the two advance-qualified misses, the aligned direction, and the selected word PC. IF can issue a ready correction on the first recovery cycle and holds the address during imem backpressure. The recovery state squashes the younger EX slot until decode issues the corrected instruction. FENCE.I forces both misses, and a registered redirect without predictor training starts the replay scrub.\n\nThe tagged successor table stores replay-index and full word-PC equality with each successor. On each accepted lookahead read, IF captures the collision decisions using the actual fill enable. An exact collision forwards the accepted bus word. A different tag at the same index blocks the old RAM result. A ready bus response still has priority and the queue issues in FIFO order.\n\nID registers the one-hot bypass masks after checking both incoming producers' write enables. The younger eligible producer takes priority. ID captures PC or the register-file value for ALU A and the immediate or register-file value for ALU B. EX uses the raw forwarded operands for branches, stores, RV32M, and RVFI. Directed tests cover both recovery directions, imem and dmem holds, replay collisions, FENCE.I, AUIPC, and trapping producers.\n\nThe recovery hold uses dmem backpressure once the corrected word arrives. Squashing EX disables the load-use and M-extension stalls during recovery. The predictor uses a full-width XNOR reduction for tag matching. IF preselects the live bus word or the last fill so the replay RAM output crosses a single data mux to the queue write port.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- Verilator lint and all eight cocotb runners passed. Co-simulation passed for selftest and all three random ELFs with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark produced the canonical CRCs in 3,540,112 marked cycles. The direct `make cosim TARGET=bench` invocation used `NRET=1` to match the core configuration.\n- The unmodified single-retirement formal suite passed 52 of 53 checks locally. Bitwuzla was still solving step 20 of `reg_ch0` at the last check. There was no counterexample or formal-driven retry.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 86.671, 88.203, and 88.203 MHz. The median gives 249.15 CoreMark iterations per second, 5.10% above the 237.06 baseline and above the 4.6% margin. Option 0 used 5,498 logic cells and 1,091 registers. Paths from the registered target miss through IF to the fetch PC and replay RAM are among the longest in placements 1 and 2.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
