{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_196","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c 'import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET; from pathlib import Path; files=list(Path(\\\"cores/bench/test\\\").glob(\\\"*.result.xml\\\")); tests=[node for path in files for node in ET.parse(path).iter(\\\"testcase\\\")]; print(len(files), \\\"suites,\\\", len(tests), \\\"tests,\\\", sum(node.find(\\\"failure\\\") is not None for node in tests), \\\"failures\\\")' && cat implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_196","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c 'import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET; from pathlib import Path; files=list(Path(\\\"cores/bench/test\\\").glob(\\\"*.result.xml\\\")); tests=[node for path in files for node in ET.parse(path).iter(\\\"testcase\\\")]; print(len(files), \\\"suites,\\\", len(tests), \\\"tests,\\\", sum(node.find(\\\"failure\\\") is not None for node in tests), \\\"failures\\\")' && cat implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"8 suites, 99 tests, 0 failures\n# Complete low-half multiplication in MEM\n\nLow-half MUL captures the forwarded operands into the shared limb registers on the EX advance edge. EX/MEM carries the result marker and the original operand values. MEM adds the three relevant 16-bit terms with carry-save compression and a 16-bit final sum, then merges the product into the existing MEM/WB write value. Consecutive MULs can capture on consecutive edges. ALTOPS uses the same launch and MEM/WB timing. High-half products and division use their existing blocking transfer schedules. The core retires through the existing single RVFI channel.\n\nThe late-result interlock uses both raw source fields and ignores x0 destinations. ID registers EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding priorities from the incoming producers' post-trap write enables, with separate masks for ALU PC and immediate inputs. A dmem hold preserves the producer values and the consumer's masks. JAL and JALR use a zero integer ALU operation and merge the link in EX.\n\nThe timing work also split ordered branch comparisons into byte lanes, used carry-save equality for relative and sequential successors, and shortened the replay-to-ID path by registering same-row collisions and selecting the instruction sources in parallel.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed in real and ALTOPS builds. All eight cocotb suites passed. The arithmetic tests cover exact low and high products, consecutive capture edges, and the high-half handshake. Pipeline tests cover dependent and independent MULs, x0, mixed multiply and divide operations, both stages' trap gating, and launch suppression during dmem holds and recovery.\n- ISS cosimulation matched selftest and all three random programs with zero-wait and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs in 3,430,915 marked cycles, 1.67% fewer than the starting core.\n- The stock ALTOPS suite passed 52 of 53 checks. `reg_ch0` is still running and has produced no counterexample.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 measured 89.077, 89.170 and 89.170 MHz. The median gives 259.90 iterations per second, 4.11% above the starting core and 0.419 MHz below the stated acceptance threshold of 89.589 MHz. The median's worst path crosses the divider's prefix trial logic.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
