{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"stat -c '%y %n' cores/bench/sim_build cores/bench/test/*.result.xml; git diff --check; git check-ignore -v cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; sed -n '1,150p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"2026-11-20 04:26:30.296983149 +0100 cores/bench/sim_build\n2026-11-20 04:26:18.452331726 +0100 cores/bench/test/test_alu_runner.result.xml\n2026-11-20 04:26:21.884442081 +0100 cores/bench/test/test_decoder_runner.result.xml\n2026-11-20 04:26:25.370579645 +0100 cores/bench/test/test_div_unit_runner.result.xml\n2026-11-20 04:26:29.433520218 +0100 cores/bench/test/test_imm_gen_runner.result.xml\n2026-11-20 04:26:30.230401691 +0100 cores/bench/test/test_pipeline_runner.result.xml\n2026-11-20 04:26:34.319554587 +0100 cores/bench/test/test_reg_file_runner.result.xml\n.gitignore:87:cores/*/implementation_notes.md\tcores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n# Registered shared multiplier\n\nThe four MUL opcodes share one signed 33 × 33 multiply fed by the forwarded EX operands. MULH sign-extends both operands, MULHSU sign-extends rs1, and the other cases zero-extend both. EX/MEM captures the low 64 product bits and the half selector under its existing enable. The pending flag is set only when a valid multiply advances through EX. MEM selects the half and writes it into the existing MEM/WB result field for forwarding, writeback, and RVFI.\n\nThe hazard unit treats a live multiply like a load and inserts one bubble on a match against either raw source field when rd is nonzero. EX/MEM forwarding excludes pending multiplies. MEM/WB retains the result when a data-memory stall clears retirement valid. All four ALTOPS formulas live in the multiplier and use the same register and forwarding path. The ALTOPS substitute is placed in both halves so the recorded half selector works in either build.\n\nThe cocotb arithmetic vectors now run through the clocked core. Multiply tests check all four opcodes across 192 operand pairs, including the original vectors. Pipeline tests cover consecutive issue, multiply-to-multiply dependencies, store data and address forwarding, x0, conservative immediate-field matches, forwarding throughout a stalled store, and wrong-path squash.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed in both hardware and ALTOPS modes.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 ALTOPS checks using the auto-selected single-retirement wrapper and config. No RTL retries were needed. Deep formal was outside this run.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all 6 cocotb runners and 65 cases.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and random1–3 with ready and stalled buses, plus the stalled CoreMark CRC check. The standalone Makefile invocation needed explicit `NRET=1` for the cosim build. A separate stalled ISS run matched all 3,154 retirements in a directed 192-pair multiply program with dependent operations, loads, stores, and wrong-path multiplies.\n- `make timing TARGET=bench` and `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` completed using the vendor Gowin flow. Formal and benchmark build outputs were staged under `$TMPDIR`.\n\n## Measured fitness\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 56.092, 55.142, and 55.142 MHz. The median was 55.142 MHz, compared with the scratch prototype's 59.457 MHz. Each placement used two DSP blocks and reported the divider remainder recurrence as its worst setup path.\n\nStalled CoreMark validated all canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 4,255,430 marker-to-marker cycles, matching the prototype and adding 0.84% over the champion. The measured fitness was 129.58 iterations/s, up 9.88% from 117.93 iterations/s and above the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
