{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'r4s1|radix-16' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Separate execute paths for memory and branch prediction\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 138.16 CoreMark iter/sec at 59.595 MHz over\\n4,313,521 marked cycles. Its reported worst setup path reaches data\\nBRAM through ID, EX and the integer ALU. A load or store always adds\\nforwarded rs1 to the decoded immediate, yet its address passes through\\nthe general ALU and the EX/MEM result mux. A scratch build with a\\nseparate address lane bypassed that mux and exposed two nearly tied\\nlimits at 58.365 MHz median. One ran from WB forwarding into the\\npredictor table write, and the other ran through two cascaded radix-8\\ndivider digits. The execute boundary needs to cut both exposed paths\\nto turn the shorter BRAM address path into a clock gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion scores 138.16 CoreMark iter/sec at 59.595 MHz over\\n4,313,521 marked cycles. Its reported worst setup path reaches data\\nBRAM through ID, EX and the integer ALU. A load or store always adds\\nforwarded rs1 to the decoded immediate, yet its address passes through\\nthe general ALU and the EX/MEM result mux. A scratch build with a\\nseparate address lane bypassed that mux and exposed two nearly tied\\nlimits at 58.365 MHz median. One ran from WB forwarding into the\\npredictor table write, and the other ran through two cascaded radix-8\\ndivider digits. The execute boundary needs to cut both exposed paths\\nto turn the shorter BRAM address path into a clock gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a full 32-bit effective address field to EX/MEM, fed directly by\\nforwarded rs1 plus the sign-extended I/S immediate. Reuse the existing\\nrs1-plus-immediate adder that computes the JALR target. Tap the raw sum\\nbefore JALR clears bit zero and before target selection. Clock the\\naddress with the existing EX/MEM hold on data-memory backpressure.\\nDrive the dmem address directly from this field. The address is live\\nonly for valid load/store requests, so its data input can sample the\\nraw sum \n…[9138 of 12210 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:93c44cbc854f1824]…\nire ALTOPS checks on the final RTL with zero failures and zero retries. Cocotb and ISS cosim exercised the real divider arithmetic.\\n- All 79 cocotb cases across seven runners passed, including the directed divider and pipeline regressions.\\n- Self-test RVFI cosim passed against the ISS in zero-wait and stalled modes. Stalled CoreMark passed all five canonical CRCs, emitted `Correct operation validated.`, and completed without out-of-bounds accesses. The start and stop markers were at cycles 15,168 and 4,328,689.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T04:18:09.903364Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 (regression, +10.78%): Registering the raw JALR adder sum as the load/store address exposed predictor-write and divider paths, so staging table writes and replacing cascaded radix-8 steps with one parallel radix-16 digit shifted the limit to EX, where parallel result selection and next-PC comparisons raised median Gowin Fmax from 61.366 to 66.022 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
