{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_80","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,85p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Bench arithmetic path shortening\n\n- MEM selects the extended load or EX register result before the MEM/WB edge. WB and both forwarding operands use the same registered value. During a dmem stall, MEM/WB retains the result, destination, and forwarding enable while clearing retirement valid.\n- EX/MEM registers the full forwarded `rs1 + imm` sum under the existing hold. MEM uses that byte address for alignment, bus requests, load extraction, store strobes, and word-aligned RVFI addresses. JALR uses the same sum and clears bit zero for control flow.\n- EX uses one-hot masks for ALU, link, multiply, and divide write values. Validity, stalls, and traps qualify side effects and M-unit handshakes. Relative, JALR, and sequential successors are compared in parallel to shorten recovery. Predictor training and redirect timing use the existing pipeline edges.\n- The divider consumes the first byte with a four-bit table lookup and parallel 13-bit differences. Constant products provide the narrow divisor multiples. The same work edge registers wide odd multiples, with even multiples derived by shifts. The next six edges each consume a nibble with fifteen parallel 37-bit differences and adjacent borrow flags. The held quotient or remainder is sign-corrected for transfer eight edges after capture. ALTOPS retains its registered result timing and formulas.\n\n## Measured result\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 73.755, 74.008, and 74.008 MHz. The 74.008 MHz median exceeds the 69.19 MHz tournament threshold. Option 0 used 4,894 LUT4s, 890 registers, 88 LUT-RAMs, four BSRAMs, and five DSPs. The divider byte prefix remains the longest reported path, with EX result capture and branch recovery close behind.\n\nThe initial implementation reached 67.001 MHz median. Indexing the nibble lookup directly, computing the narrow multiples separately, and selecting and comparing the three next-PC candidates in parallel raised the measured clock. The hypothesis scratch build reached 77.260 MHz median and used seven DSPs.\n\nStalled CoreMark scored **171.57 iterations/s**, an **11.88%** gain over 153.35 iterations/s. It ran ten iterations in **4,313,521 marked cycles** between cycles 15,168 and 4,328,689, matched all five canonical CRCs, emitted `Correct operation validated.`, and completed with no out-of-bounds accesses.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint passed in both real-arithmetic and ALTOPS configurations. The single-channel RVFI contract precheck passed.\n- All 81 cocotb cases across seven runners passed. New coverage includes prefix cutoffs, exact-multiple boundaries, a stalled byte store and dependent signed byte load with forwarded bases and negative offsets, and JALR bit-zero clearing. The divider tests retain exact transfer timing and deferred acceptance checks.\n- Self-test and all three random programs matched the ISS with zero waits and with both buses stalled. `make fpga TARGET=bench` passed CoreMark validation and all three placements.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the final RTL with zero failures and zero fix attempts. The register consistency check took about nine minutes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
