{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short --untracked-files=all && cat implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_div_unit.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/rtl/alu_one_hot.sv\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Register arithmetic and forwarding selects before use\n\nID compares both raw source tags against the nonzero destinations in EX and MEM. The existing forwarding priority logic qualifies those matches with each producer's next write eligibility and registers the resulting masks in ID/EX. EX checks link alignment using the JAL immediate or the forwarded JALR sum. MEM uses the same misalignment check for forwarding eligibility and writeback. The existing dmem hold retains both the masks and producer values while MEM/WB clears retirement valid. The M units and RVFI operand registers capture the forwarded operands at the request handshake.\n\nID also registers the 11 RV32I operation enables. EX combines the masked integer datapath, link, multiply, and divide results with an OR. The standalone encoded ALU wraps the same integer datapath. The divider captures both prefix cutoff flags directly from the request divisor and signedness. The negative cutoff excludes exact magnitudes 16 and 256 by testing for a nonzero low slice.\n\nThe divider keeps the existing prefix mux structure. A trial that folded the byte cutoff into each quotient digit mask reduced median Fmax to 73.319 MHz, so the implementation retains the registered cutoff as the mux select.\n\nThe directed tests cover misaligned JAL, JALR, and load producers with older writes to the same register. Divider coverage includes signed quotient and remainder on both sides of the negative cutoffs, and changes the divisor input after launch to check capture.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All 84 cocotb cases passed.\n- Co-simulation matched the ISS on selftest and the three random programs with and without both bus stalls. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC checks and took 4,313,521 marked cycles for 10 iterations.\n- Gowin options 0, 1, and 2 placed at 73.993, 76.909, and 76.909 MHz with 4,928 LUT4s. The 76.909 MHz median gives 178.30 CoreMark iterations per second, a 3.92% gain over the champion. The worst setup path passes through the forwarded branch target in option 0 and the divider byte prefix in options 1 and 2.\n- The unmodified single-retirement ALTOPS suite has passed 52 of 53 checks. Its register consistency check is still running at step 20 and has reported no counterexample. A separate run passed the same step-20 assertions with unconstrained arithmetic results, load data, predicted successors, and branch conditions. This allows every behavior of the original model and checks register consistency across arbitrary values at those points.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
