{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_130","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat implementation_notes.md && tail -n 4 \"$TMPDIR/div-lanes-test-final.log\" && tail -n 5 \"$TMPDIR/div-lanes-cosim-final.log\"'","aggregated_output":"# Radix-16 and radix-8 divider\n\n- The request edge classifies the raw divisor at magnitudes 16, 256, and 4096 while converting both operands to magnitudes. It captures the narrow lane's first-nibble table result and byte cutoff.\n- The narrow lane finishes the byte prefix on the setup edge, then consumes six four-bit digits with 17-bit trial subtracts and a 12-bit residual. The wide lane uses three 17-bit prefix trials over the top 14 dividend bits, then consumes six three-bit digits. Its seven work trials reject 35-bit multiples with an upper bit set or a borrow from the low 32 bits.\n- The lanes share the dividend and quotient register, the partial remainder register, and the low bits of the odd multiples through 7D. Division by zero holds the captured dividend magnitude in the shift register. The completion mux returns the signed-corrected dividend for remainder and all ones for quotient.\n- Results can transfer on the eighth edge after capture. The completed datapath holds its state under backpressure. Signed overflow produces the RV32M quotient and remainder through ordinary magnitude and sign correction.\n- The divider tests exercise the signed cutoffs, every wide prefix digit, wide multiples crossing 2^32, division by zero, signed overflow, and backpressure. They also change the input operands and opcode after capture.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all seven cocotb runners passed, including all nine divider tests. The preprocessed ALTOPS divider matched the baseline byte for byte.\n- ISS co-simulation passed for selftest and all three random programs, with and without memory stalls. Stalled CoreMark validated its CRCs in 3,758,572 marker-bracketed cycles for ten iterations.\n- Gowin options