y load still holds EX through the existing\\ndmem stall. Retain the\n27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Resolve load-dependent equality branches in a late branch lane\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"A profile of the 3,540,112-cycle champion found 203,924 load-use\\nbubbles, including 135,260 before BEQ or BNE. An equality branch can\\nissue\n…[1886 of 4958 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b892356ca7ddd76d]…\ntance at 84.28 MHz. Covering dependent addresses with the same\\nlate operand path gives the design more timing headroom.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion delivers 237.06 CoreMark iter/sec at 83.921 MHz over\\n3,540,112 marked cycles. Of the measured load-use interlocks, 135,260\\npreceded BEQ or BNE and 59,266 reflected a true load-to-load address\\ndependency. Every producer in those load pairs was LW. Their dependent\\nloads used immediate offsets of 0, 1, or 2. Round 9 saved 3.88% of\\ncycles by letting equality branches issue behind loads, and fell short\\nof acceptance at 84.28 MHz. Covering dependent addresses with the same\\nlate operand path gives the design more timing headroom.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Let validated BEQ and BNE issue immediately behind a load, and let\\nvalidated loads with unsigned offsets 0 through 3 issue immediately\\nbehind LW. Admit the fast paths only for real nonzero destination\\ndependencies. Keep the normal interlock for other consumers. Qualify\\nload producers with effect\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
