ontrol transfers. On accepted fetch, use the same tagged\\nsuccessor for the normal PC update and synchronous replay lookup.\\nPreserve the registered recovery squash, full-width EX validation,\\nready-bus priority, collision forwarding, and RVFI successor. Let an\\naccepted external fill finish on the invalidation edge since the\\npending flag masks stale replay afterward and the scrub \n…[4084 of 7156 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a350ba8ec347edea]…\nc, 2.68% above the cited champion and below the 87.783 MHz acceptance threshold. Placement option 0 used 5,369 logic cells, 1,047 registers, and six block RAMs. Options 1 and 2 were limited by replay collision forwarding through instruction delivery and register-file reads into ID.\\n- Local single-channel ALTOPS formal was incomplete within the implementation window. The one run passed 52 of 53 checks. Bitwuzla was still solving step 20 of `reg_ch0` and produced no counterexample. There were no formal-driven RTL fixes or retries.\\n\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T11:11:50.439262Z\",\n  \"slot\": 2,\n  \"round_id\": 9,\n  \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r9s2 (regression, +2.68%): Registering the selected target or fall-through before the tagged successor lookup shortened the path to the replay RAM address, leaving replay collision forwarding through instruction delivery and register-file reads to cap median Gowin Fmax at 86.171 MHz against the 87.783 MHz acceptance threshold at the same CoreMark cycle count.\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
