{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_154","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat implementation_notes.md && git status --short && tail -5 \"$TMPDIR/bench-mul-reg-yices.output\" && date -u'","aggregated_output":"# Adjacent low MUL consumers\n\nLow MUL captures its limbs on the EX advance edge and recombines them at MEM/WB. Independent low multiplies can launch on consecutive edges. High-half multiplication and division retain their blocking schedules. ALTOPS uses the same launch and completion boundaries.\n\nID records the matching nonzero source tags and selects legal register ADD or store-data consumers. A paired ADD completes in MEM using the registered product and the other forwarded operand. A double match produces the product shifted left by one bit. A dependent successor waits for the ADD to enter MEM/WB. A paired store uses the product for data and the normal EX address calculation for an independent base. The product stays available through a dmem hold after retirement valid clears. Other true dependencies on low MUL interlock.\n\nThe integer and auxiliary EX result registers are ORed for EX/MEM forwarding. The paired ADD reuses the store-data payload for its non-product operand. ID registers forwarding priority and result classes so the EX data paths use preselected sources. The divider prefix decodes its small divisor in parallel with the dividend lookup. Fetch registers both recovery addresses alongside the resolved direction to remove the wide address mux from the branch comparison path. Registered recovery suppresses wrong-path launches, memory effects, and retirement. RVFI reports actual product values and preserves the existing raw-tag convention on channel 0.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passes in real arithmetic and ALTOPS modes. All eight cocotb runners pass, including the cycle-level MUL pairing tests. ISS cosimulation passes selftest and all three random workloads with and without bus stalls, plus the stalled CoreMark CRC gate. A Yosys equivalence check passes for the revised real-arithmetic divider against the original divider.\n- The initial formal run passed 52 of 53 checks. `reg_ch0` found an `LBU` writing x31 immediately before a trapping word whose raw rs1 field was x31. The implementation retains the load's raw-tag interlock and supplies raw RVFI tags that match a preceding low MUL or paired ADD from MEM/WB. The current ALTOPS run has 52 passes, including liveness, ADD, MUL, and SW. Bitwuzla is still solving `reg_ch0`.\n- Stalled CoreMark completed in 3,395,412 marked cycles, a 2.69% reduction, with all five canonical CRCs and no out-of-bounds access.\n- Gowin options 0, 1, and 2 completed at 84.410, 89.587, and 89.587 MHz. The median of 89.587 MHz yields 263.85 iterations/sec and falls short of the 93.329 MHz acceptance threshold. Replay and its issue feedback are the longest setup paths. The paired MEM ADD and store-data paths are outside the 25 longest setup paths in all three placements.\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mul_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_mul_unit.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n##   0:00:00  Skipping step 19..\n##   0:00:00  Checking assumptions in step 20..\n##   0:01:00  waiting for solver (1 minute)\n##   0:05:00  waiting for solver (5 minutes)\n##   0:05:54  Checking assertions in step 20..\nFri Nov 20 16:28:47 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
