{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_123","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat implementation_notes.md; rg '\"'^(place_option|score Fmax)'\"' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/bench-timing-registered.log\"'","aggregated_output":"# Bench deferred operands\n\nLow MUL captures partial products in EX and writes its low word at MEM/WB. A local load/MUL register supplies a paired register ADD, store write data, or load-dependent equality branch. ADD and store pairs use the MUL result. Other raw-tag dependencies interlock for one cycle, including a dependent memory address, any consumer of a paired ADD, and a word load compared with a different nonzero register. High MUL and DIV/REM retain their blocking transfer schedules.\n\nDeferred equality saves the older forwarding or register-file fallback in EX. MEM uses the post-alignment load write decision, the load-zero flag for word comparisons with x0, and the actual extension bit for narrow comparisons. It reports the resolved operands and successor on RVFI channel 0. On a late miss, the first correction edge releases the saved branch retirement, annuls the two younger slots, and cancels a speculative M launch.\n\nFetch carries decoded pair classes with queue and replay words and compares source tags before selecting the queued, bus, or replay word. The write pointer advances on each accepted fetch. EX validates the uncorrected head or fetch PC. The older verdict takes redirect priority, and the predictor's single write port gives the older training packet priority when both packets are valid.\n\nThe directed tests cover dependent DIV, corrected DIV after a speculative DIV request, held store data, unsupported address dependencies, load-trap fallback, signed and unsigned halfword equality, both-source aliases, and simultaneous redirects with and without FENCE.I replay invalidation.\n\n## Verification\n\nVerilator lint, cocotb, and ISS co-simulation pass. `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` checked selftest and the three random programs with both ready and stalled buses. The stalled CoreMark run validated all canonical CRCs in 3,262,625 marked cycles, down 6.49% from 3,489,128.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 86.188, 86.019, and 86.019 MHz. The 86.019 MHz median gives 263.65 CoreMark iterations per second and falls short of the 89.679 MHz promotion threshold. The leading paths reach ID through corrected fetch and reach the ALU through the ordinary MEM/WB forwarding selects. The retained design registers EX/MEM ALU forwarding eligibility, keeps the paired ADD behind MEM/WB, and computes the corrected fetch increment in parallel with redirect selection. A trial with byte-split execute adders was slower and was reverted.\n\nThe first run of the unchanged single-retirement formal gate found BEQ/BNE counterexamples involving unsigned halfword values with bit 15 set. For example, a zero-extended load of 0x00008008 compared equal to 0xFFFF8008 and sent a BEQ down a misaligned target. The fix uses the actual extension in bit 16 to choose the saved upper-bit equality check. Both checks pass on the retry, along with liveness, unique retirement, forward and backward PC consistency, and illegal-instruction handling. The full retry remained incomplete within the local run window.\nplace_option 0: Fmax 86.188 MHz, 8 logic levels; logic 6482, registers 1261, LUT-RAM 161, BSRAM 6, DSP 5\nplace_option 1: Fmax 86.019 MHz, 13 logic levels; logic 6482, registers 1261, LUT-RAM 161, BSRAM 6, DSP 5\nplace_option 2: Fmax 86.019 MHz, 13 logic levels; logic 6482, registers 1261, LUT-RAM 161, BSRAM 6, DSP 5\nscore Fmax (median): 86.019 MHz\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
