{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short && sed -n '1,120p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Elastic instruction fetch\n\nFetch now holds one instruction and its PC in a fall-through buffer. The registered full bit selects the decoder and register-file input. An empty buffer accepts a ready word during a backend hold or load-use bubble. A full buffer can issue while imem is stalled and can drain and refill on the same edge. The bus PC advances when fetch accepts a word.\n\nID/EX captures the decoded payload whenever EX can accept a new entry. Fetch availability, load-use, and redirect determine the entry's validity. A valid aligned branch or jump redirects on the edge it advances into EX/MEM. That edge invalidates the buffer and the next ID/EX entry and installs the target PC. Invalid EX entries and divider bubbles clear the registered writeback and memory enables. The existing divider handshake, misalignment handling, and MEM/WB forwarding across a data stall are preserved.\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed pipeline behavior. A shared data-stall signal drives the EX/MEM hold and the MEM/WB payload hold because both respond to the same outstanding request. The load-use interlock continues to compare the two raw source fields.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint passed in both the hardware and ALTOPS configurations.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the first run. No retries were needed.\n- All six cocotb runners passed, including the new drain/refill and stalled-branch tests. They check issue through imem stalls, the redirect and first ready target fetch, stable store requests and exact accepted writes, and contiguous RVFI order.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` passed selftest and three random traces with each bus-stall mode, plus stalled CoreMark with the canonical CRCs. The local Makefile invocation requires `NRET=1` for the bench core, which the orchestrator already supplies.\n\n## FPGA and CoreMark\n\n| Metric | Champion | Elastic fetch | Change |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Median Gowin Fmax | 45.180 MHz | 49.878 MHz | +10.40% |\n| Stalled CoreMark cycles between markers | 4,491,485 | 4,353,657 | −3.07% |\n| CoreMark iterations per second | 100.59 | 114.57 | +13.90% |\n\n`make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1` measured 48.183, 49.878, and 49.878 MHz across placement options 0, 1, and 2. The option 0 worst path spans 15 reported cells from the register file through the EX ALU to the data memory. The champion's 49-cell redirect-to-ID/EX path is absent from the new worst-path reports. Placements 1 and 2 also show divider paths close to their worst delay. The buffer increased the reported Gowin logic count from 2,971 to 3,059 and the register count from 632 to 697.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
