{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_69","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short --untracked-files=all && sed -n '1,150p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_div_unit.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Bench execute path separation\n\n## RTL and tests\n\n- EX/MEM registers the raw forwarded `rs1 + imm` sum under the existing data-memory hold. JALR masks bit zero only on its control-target path. MEM uses the registered address for the bus, alignment, byte lanes, load extraction and word-aligned RVFI reporting.\n- IF samples the training source PC and pending bit alongside the existing recovery target. The next edge writes the tagged entry from the prior sampled pair. Reset clears pending training and entry validity. Fetch lookup and registered redirect timing retain the existing policy.\n- The blocking divider uses a nibble lookup and a byte-wide trial on its prefix edge, then one parallel radix-16 digit on each of six edges. Odd multiples are prepared on the prefix edge from the captured divisor. Sign correction uses the held quotient or remainder, and the result still transfers eight edges after capture. ALTOPS retains its existing formulas and transfer timing.\n- The directed tests cover every first nibble for divisors 0 through 16, quotient and remainder around the byte-prefix limit, a stalled byte store and dependent load using forwarded bases with negative offsets, and back-to-back training of distinct JAL and JALR successors.\n\n## Timing adjustments and results\n\nThe initial implementation reached 61.366 MHz median and was limited by the integer result path. EX now selects integer, link, multiply and divide results in parallel on the same writeback lane. EX also compares the relative and JALR candidates with the fetch PC, then selects the comparison result using the decoded target type. These changes shorten the result and redirect logic while retaining the existing forwarding priority and branch behavior.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 63.528, 66.022 and 66.022 MHz. Option 0 used 4,837 logic cells, 980 registers, 64 LUT-RAMs, six BSRAMs and two DSPs. The worst paths at the median are redirect capture and integer result capture. The median Fmax and 4,313,521 marked CoreMark cycles yield **153.06 iterations/s**, a **10.78%** gain over the supplied 138.16 iterations/s baseline.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint passed for the real arithmetic and ALTOPS builds.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the final RTL with zero failures and zero retries. Cocotb and ISS cosim exercised the real divider arithmetic.\n- All 79 cocotb cases across seven runners passed, including the directed divider and pipeline regressions.\n- Self-test RVFI cosim passed against the ISS in zero-wait and stalled modes. Stalled CoreMark passed all five canonical CRCs, emitted `Correct operation validated.`, and completed without out-of-bounds accesses. The start and stop markers were at cycles 15,168 and 4,328,689.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_70","type":"agent_message","text":"Implemented the separate memory address lane, deferred predictor training, and eight-edge radix-16 divider in `cores/bench`. EX also selects writeback results in parallel and compares each branch target before target selection.\n\nGowin measured **66.022 MHz median**, yielding **153.06 CoreMark iterations/s** over 4,313,521 marked cycles, a **10.78% gain**. Verilator lint, all 53 formal checks, all 79 cocotb cases, ISS cosim, and stalled CoreMark CRC validation passed.\n\n[Implementation notes](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261120-002-r4s1/implementation_notes.md)"}}
