{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_73","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && git diff --check && sed -n '1,70p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_div_unit.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Split divider feedback by divisor magnitude\n\n- `cores/bench/rtl/div_unit.sv` captures the unsigned divisor magnitude and selects a 12-bit or 32-bit remainder register. The narrow path consumes 2 bits and then six groups of 5. The wide path consumes 14 bits and then six groups of 3, including the 12 leading zero quotient bits in the prefix. Both paths share the dividend/quotient register, divisor multiples, and phase counter.\n- A parameterized digit circuit implements the radix-4 and radix-8 steps with parallel subtractions and adjacent borrow flags. The shared register's mutually exclusive input selects are implemented with masks to keep selection parallel.\n- Division by zero loads the architectural result into the dividend/quotient register at capture. The real divider selects and sign-corrects the held registers during the valid cycle. ALTOPS retains the original capture and final-edge output registers. Results become valid seven edges after capture, can transfer at edge eight, and hold under backpressure. Reset cancels the pending transaction.\n- The divider tests cover the magnitude thresholds, prefix digit boundaries, exact and adjacent multiples, maximum magnitudes, changing inputs after capture, backpressure, and reset during work or a held result.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint passed for real arithmetic and ALTOPS. All seven bench unit-test runners passed, including the six divider tests.\n- The single-channel co-simulation passed for selftest and the three random traces with and without bus stalls. CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs with both stalls enabled in 4,313,521 marked cycles.\n- A Yosys SAT proof with a reset base case and temporal induction proved that the ALTOPS divider's outputs match the champion cycle for cycle under arbitrary requests and result backpressure.\n- Local `formal/run_all.sh` is in progress. The first run completed 52 passing checks and is still solving `reg_ch0`.\n- Gowin measured 67.669, 72.226, and 72.226 MHz, for a median of 72.226 MHz and 167.44 CoreMark iterations per second. The mapped design uses 4,295 LUT4s and 766 registers. The LUT4 count is 68 above the scratch result. The worst setup paths cross EX forwarding into the predictor.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
