{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()[-8:]:\n    row = json.loads(line)\n    print(row['id'],row['implementation_notes'][-1100:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 \nhyp-20261120-003-r3s2 ID is held. RVFI reports the architectural successor.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Strict Verilator lint and all six cocotb runners passed, including all 17 pipeline cases. The pipeline tests cover draining under imem stalls with a queued load-use dependency, squashing buffered and held wrong-path stores, and cross-page recovery and sequential fetch.\n- Formal passed all 53 checks on the first retry, including `reg_ch0` and both PC continuity checks. The initial `pc_fwd_ch0` counterexample showed an incorrect queue head after a divider hold. Yosys had elaborated the unpacked array of packed structs as one 64-bit word. Declaring the entries explicitly as 64-bit vectors corrected the elaboration.\n- With `NRET=1`, cosim passed selftest and all three random traces with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark produced the canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 4,008,388 marker-bracketed cycles.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 48.680, 49.663, and 49.663 MHz. Option 0 used 3,846 LUT4s. The median gives 123.90 iterations/sec, 0.55 iterations/sec above the 123.35 threshold.\n\nhyp-20261120-001-r4s0 o the divider. The tests cover both read ports in both banks, clearing previously written rows with reset, write-through, x0, youngest-writer priority, and both operands forwarding from an already-retired load across a data-memory hold.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. The RVFI channel-0 contract precheck and all seven cocotb runners passed.\n- Cosim passed for selftest and the three random programs with and without bus stalls, and for CoreMark with bus stalls. The local Makefile build uses `NRET=1` to match `core.yaml`.\n- The three Gowin placements reached 63.858, 66.149, and 66.149 MHz. The build reports 4,272 LUT4s, 784 registers, 88 LUT-RAM cells, and four BRAMs. The register file uses 32 of the RAM16SDP4 cells, covering both copies of each bank.\n- CoreMark validated all five canonical CRCs in 4,313,521 marked cycles. The 66.149 MHz median ex\n…[3712 of 6784 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:23cf31e9e5fe81f9]…\ngle-channel co-simulation passed for selftest and the three random traces with and without bus stalls. CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs with both stalls enabled in 4,313,521 marked cycles.\n- The preprocessed ALTOPS RTL matches the champion after stripping whitespace and comments. A Yosys SAT proof with a reset base case and temporal induction verified output equality under arbitrary requests and result backpressure.\n- Standalone SAT checks proved `trial = divisor × digit + remainder` and `remainder < divisor` for every legal trial in each of the four digit configurations.\n- Local `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL, including `reg_ch0`.\n- Gowin measured 67.669, 72.226, and 72.226 MHz, for a median of 72.226 MHz and 167.44 CoreMark iterations per second, 9.19% above the champion. The mapped design uses 4,295 LUT4s and 766 registers. The LUT4 count is 68 above the scratch result. The worst setup paths cross EX forwarding into the predictor.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r5s2 \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
