{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'ls -ld /tmp /private/tmp; ls -ld /opt/gowin/IDE/lib; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md | tail -n 15; ls -la /srv/hwebench/tmp/4876824c/d0ce49f2'", "aggregated_output": "drwxrwxrwt 161 nobody nogroup 3280 Nov 20 09:30 /tmp\nls: cannot access '/private/tmp': No such file or directory\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 nobody nogroup 4096 Jul 10  2025 /opt/gowin/IDE/lib\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +9.88%): Registering the shared DSP product in EX/MEM exposed the divider’s three cascaded radix-4 steps as the critical path, capping median Gowin Fmax at 55.142 MHz and leaving CoreMark throughput behind the round winner’s design with two radix-8 steps per cycle.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 (improvement, +17.15%): With the divider recurrence shortened, registering the multiplier’s 16-bit limb products removed the DSP-to-data-BRAM address path, and carry-save recombination kept CoreMark’s 93,960 low-half MULs to one extra EX cycle each, letting the clock gain outweigh the added stalls.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (regression, +5.06%): A two-entry fall-through queue let the tagged next-PC predictor fetch through load-use bubbles and data-bus holds, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.02%, with a median Gowin Fmax of 49.663 MHz against the 55.38 MHz needed at that cycle count to match the round-winning RV32M lane.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 (improvement, +10.99%): With asynchronous LUT RAM reads and bypass matches latched at ID/EX, forwarding still appeared among Gowin's worst paths until folding the AUIPC PC and immediate selects into one-hot operand masks removed the serial EX mux, raising median Fmax from 59.994 to 66.149 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 (regression, +10.78%): Registering the raw JALR adder sum as the load/store address exposed predictor-write and divider paths, so staging table writes and replacing cascaded radix-8 steps with one parallel radix-16 digit shifted the limit to EX, where parallel result selection and next-PC comparisons raised median Gowin Fmax from 61.366 to 66.022 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 (regression, +7.78%): Fetching through multiply and bus hold\n…[2948 of 6020 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9e033cf281b774ca]…\nigit per edge preserved the eight-edge transfer schedule and shifted Gowin's timing limit to EX result and recovery paths, raising median Fmax from 77.436 to 83.877 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles for 455 extra logic cells.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r7s2 (improvement, +9.39%): Replaying learned BTB successor words during instruction-bus stalls cut CoreMark cycles by 6.39%, and writing every accepted word into a FIFO slot free at cycle start, even on decode bypass, removed decode acceptance from the RAM write enable and helped reach 79.292 MHz median Gowin Fmax.\ntotal 24\ndrwxrwx---+  7 nobody    nogroup   4096 Nov 20 09:32 .\ndrwxrwx---+  5 nobody    nogroup   4096 Nov 20 09:31 ..\ndr-xr-xr-x   2 hwebench2 hwebench2   40 Nov 20 09:32 .agents\ndr-xr-xr-x   2 hwebench2 hwebench2   40 Nov 20 09:32 .aws\ndr-xr-xr-x   2 hwebench2 hwebench2   40 Nov 20 09:32 .codex\ndr-xr-xr-x   2 hwebench2 hwebench2   40 Nov 20 09:32 .git\ndrwxrwx---+ 12 hwebench2 hwebench2 4096 Nov 20 09:32 codex-bwrap-synthetic-mount-targets-1002\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
