{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_191","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'from tools.orchestrator import allowed_patterns_for,offlimits_changes; print(offlimits_changes(\\\".\\\", allowed_patterns_for(\\\"bench\\\")))'\ndiff -qr cores/bench/rtl \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/bench-rtl-pc\"\ngit diff --check\ncat implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"mkdir -p failed for path /srv/hwebench/homes/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep2/.config/matplotlib: [Errno 30] Read-only file system: '/srv/hwebench/homes/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep2/.config'\nMatplotlib created a temporary cache directory at /srv/hwebench/tmp/4876824c/6206a823/matplotlib-z2t7p5tj because there was an issue with the default path (/srv/hwebench/homes/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep2/.config/matplotlib); it is highly recommended to set the MPLCONFIGDIR environment variable to a writable directory, in particular to speed up the import of Matplotlib and to better support multiprocessing.\n[]\n# Adjacent load-value prediction in bench\n\n- Added a 128 × 32 synchronous load history indexed by effective data address bits `[8:2]`. A nine-bit EX sum supplies ordinary load indices. Speculative pointer loads reuse the predicted word index. The table holds its output across dmem stalls, scrubs after reset, and skips retirement writes that collide with a lookup.\n- Fetch predecodes legal BEQ/BNE and loads with offsets 0–3 for live, replayed, and queued words. EX uses registered producer matches. Pointer speculation requires an adjacent LW and a base-register match. The ordinary load-use interlock still covers raw rs2-only dependencies.\n- MEM registers grouped comparisons with the formatted load response. A branch accepts when its guessed and real equality results agree. A dependent load requires an exact, aligned base. Producer traps and rejected successors trigger the same annulment and retry. Accepted consumers report the preceding load’s real writeback value on their affected RVFI operands.\n- Validation gates backend side effects immediately. Fetch handles the retry on the following cycle, holds the consumer PC through instruction-bus stalls, and retains replay entries. Failed instructions cannot train either predictor.\n- Directed tests exercise equality preservation and mismatch, successor rejection, both raw operand fields, crossed bus stalls, exact-base and alignment failures, chained loads, producer faults, and retained replay.\n\n## Timing changes\n\n- Fetch computes the load-use checks for the buffered and incoming words in parallel. ID registers the ordinary forwarding masks alongside the raw EX/MEM matches. Only the separate branch comparison and pointer address use the predicted data.\n- The retry edge writes the consumer PC into the fetch PC register. The held-retry state masks target prediction and replay until the bus accepts that instruction. Ordinary corrections use their existing address register.\n\n## Local results\n\n- Verilator lint passed. Co-simulation passed selftest and all three random traces with zero wait states and with both memory buses stalled. CoreMark passed its canonical CRC checks.\n- Stalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,303,482 marked cycles, down 5.32% from 3,489,128. At the measured median clock, throughput was 268.51 iterations per second.\n- Gowin placements reached 81.752, 88.703, and 88.703 MHz with seven block RAMs. Median Fmax was 88.703 MHz, below the 90.80 MHz promotion threshold.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
