{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_70","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short --untracked-files=all && git check-ignore -v cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '1,100p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n.gitignore:87:cores/*/implementation_notes.md\tcores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n# Tagged last-next-PC predictor\n\n## Implementation\n\n- IF indexes a 64-entry table with PC[7:2] and checks its 24-bit tag. Each unreset 54-bit entry holds the tag and word-aligned successor. A resettable valid vector gates the asynchronous lookup. The payload has one synchronous write port.\n- EX trains the table at the instruction's PC on every valid, non-trapping branch, JAL, or JALR advance. A not-taken branch writes its fall-through. EX compares the resolved successor with the registered fetch PC on every valid advance and uses the same successor for RVFI. JAL and JALR retain their PC+4 link, and traps resolve to PC+4.\n- Data-memory backpressure holds ID/EX and the fetch PC and defers correction and training. When EX advances with a mismatch, the correction takes precedence over the fetch hold and the existing hazard unit squashes the younger slot.\n- IF stores the fetch PC in word units. EX shares the PC-relative target calculation between branches and JAL and applies the alignment check to the selected control-transfer target. A guarded include in `core.sv` lets the requested Verilator glob command see the existing type declarations in source order.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with the requested command.\n- The single-retirement `formal/run_all.sh` suite passed all 53 checks with zero retries.\n- All five cocotb runners passed, including new pipeline checks for warm taken and fall-through predictions, tag aliases, and a stale prediction that fetches a wrong-path store.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` passed selftest and all three random traces against the ISS with and without both bus stalls. An alternating-JALR loop with stores at both targets also matched the ISS under both stalls for five seeds. The explicit `NRET=1` selects the existing single-channel C++ build.\n- Stalled CoreMark finished in 4,220,145 marker-bracketed cycles, down 6.04% from 4,491,485, with all five canonical CRCs and the validation message.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 5.065, 5.546, and 5.546 MHz. Option 0 used 12,446 LUT4s and eight BSRAMs. The 5.546 MHz median yields 13.14 CoreMark iterations/sec, up 8.43% from 12.12.\n\n## Limitations\n\nDifferent PCs sharing an index can evict each other, and alternating outcomes or targets can repeatedly mispredict. A stale entry at a PC whose instruction has changed to a non-control instruction can continue to predict until overwritten. EX corrects each mismatch before the younger instruction can issue. The worst local Gowin paths still run through EX forwarding and the ALU into the data-memory path.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
