{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"tail -2 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c -4000; ls /srv/hwebench/toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin | rg 'abc\"'$|bitwuzla|boolector|btormc|yices|z3|smtbmc'\"'; sed -n '118,185p' tools/eval/formal.py; git status --short; git diff --check\"", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Split divider feedback by divisor magnitude\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 153.35 CoreMark iterations per second at a\\nmedian 66.149 MHz over 4,313,521 marked cycles. Gowin's reported\\n20.954 ns, 70-cell setup path runs through two dependent full-width\\nradix-8 steps in the divider. A small divisor bounds the remainder\\nwidth. A large divisor bounds the number of quotient bits. Using\\nboth bounds can shorten the feedback while preserving the eight-edge\\ndivider protocol and the existing EX issue schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 153.35 CoreMark iterations per second at a\\nmedian 66.149 MHz over 4,313,521 marked cycles. Gowin's reported\\n20.954 ns, 70-cell setup path runs through two dependent full-width\\nradix-8 steps in the divider. A small divisor bounds the remainder\\nwidth. A large divisor bounds the number of quotient bits. Using\\nboth bounds can shorten the feedback while preserving the eight-edge\\ndivider protocol and the existing EX issue schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the blocking divider into two remainder datapaths selected by the\\ncaptured unsigned divisor magnitude. Share the 32-bit dividend/quotient\\nshift register, divisor multiples, phase counter and result interface.\\nAt capture, choose the narrow path for divisor magnitudes below 4096\\nand the wide path otherwise.\\n\\nFor a nonzero narrow divisor, the partial remainder is strictly less\\nthan the divisor and fits in 12 bits. On the first work edge, consume\\nthe top tw