{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_20", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/core.yaml && tail -15 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && sed -n '1,165p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "name: bench\ndescription: Frozen LLM-benchmark fixture core. Identical RTL to baseline at the bench-fixture-v1\n  freeze point. The benchmark matrix clones from the bench-fixture-v1 orphan branch\n  and operates only on this directory.\nisa: rv32im\nnret: 1\ntarget_fpga: tang-nano-20k\ntargets:\n  fmax_mhz: 135\n  lut4: 10000\n  coremark_iter_s: 300\n  coremark_per_mhz: 2.2\ncurrent:\n  fmax_mhz: 79.292\n  lut4: 7539\n  ff: 1104\n  coremark_iter_s: 3.0e-06\n  coremark_per_mhz: 0.0\n  source_id: hyp-20261120-003-r7s2\n  updated: '2026-11-20T08:11:27.290823Z'\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Register a shared DSP multiplier product before result selection\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"With redirect recovery registered, the champion reaches 49.767 MHz and\\n117.93 CoreMark iterations per second. The median Gowin placement reports\\na 25.388 ns arrival from the register-file block RAM through the unsigned\\nmultiply DSP and six result-selection LUTs to a data-memory block RAM.\\nThe four multiply operations currently share the same EX result path as\\ninteger operations and effective addresses. Registering the DSP output\\nseparately can cut that path while keeping integer and memory latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"With redirect recovery registered, the champion reaches 49.767 MHz and\\n117.93 CoreMark iterations per second. The median Gowin placement reports\\na 25.388 ns arrival from the register-file block RAM through the unsigned\\nmultiply DSP and six result-selection LUTs to a data-memory block RAM.\\nThe four multiply operations currently share the same EX result path as\\ninteger operations and effective addresses. Registering the DSP output\\nseparately can cut that path while keeping integer and memory latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Feed one signed 33-by-33 multiply directly from the two forwarded EX\\noperands. Extend rs1 with its sign for MULH and MULHSU, and extend rs2\\nwith its sign for MULH. Zero-extend each operand in the other cases.\\nCapture the low 64 product bits in the EX/MEM register along with a v\n…[154626 of 157698 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f0d08cd22144fe18]…\nion, +5.69%): Capturing the divider's first quotient digit and partial remainder at request launch removes the byte prefix from the first work edge while preserving the eight-edge transfer, leaving the forwarded JALR address into IF recovery to cap median Gowin Fmax at 81.844 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r7s1 (regression, +8.32%): Choosing the prefix length by divisor magnitude and using parallel trial subtracts for one radix-32 or radix-8 digit per edge preserved the eight-edge transfer schedule and shifted Gowin's timing limit to EX result and recovery paths, raising median Fmax from 77.436 to 83.877 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles for 455 extra logic cells.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r7s2 (improvement, +9.39%): Replaying learned BTB successor words during instruction-bus stalls cut CoreMark cycles by 6.39%, and writing every accepted word into a FIFO slot free at cycle start, even on decode bypass, removed decode acceptance from the RAM write enable and helped reach 79.292 MHz median Gowin Fmax.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
