{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_21", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'divider|prefix|widelogic|90.56|86.822' cores/bench/LESSONS.md | tail -13\"", "aggregated_output": "2:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.42%): Avoiding redirects with a 64-entry tagged next-PC table cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.04%, with the combinational EX divider still limiting median Gowin Fmax to 5.546 MHz and leaving throughput well behind the round winner, whose clocked divider raised Fmax to 45.180 MHz.\n6:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +9.88%): Registering the shared DSP product in EX/MEM exposed the divider’s three cascaded radix-4 steps as the critical path, capping median Gowin Fmax at 55.142 MHz and leaving CoreMark throughput behind the round winner’s design with two radix-8 steps per cycle.\n7:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 (improvement, +17.15%): With the divider recurrence shortened, registering the multiplier’s 16-bit limb products removed the DSP-to-data-BRAM address path, and carry-save recombination kept CoreMark’s 93,960 low-half MULs to one extra EX cycle each, letting the clock gain outweigh the added stalls.\n10:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 (regression, +10.78%): Registering the raw JALR adder sum as the load/store address exposed predictor-write and divider paths, so staging table writes and replacing cascaded radix-8 steps with one parallel radix-16 digit shifted the limit to EX, where parallel result selection and next-PC comparisons raised median Gowin Fmax from 61.366 to 66.022 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n14:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r5s2 (regression, +9.87%): A page-local predictor with two-bit direction counters feeding a LUT RAM fetch queue cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 7.91%, with the divider’s divisor-to-remainder path capping median Gowin Fmax at 66.933 MHz and leaving it short of the 68.16 MHz needed to match the round winner at that cycle count.\n15:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r6s0 (regression, +3.92%): Registering producer-qualified forwarding masks, one-hot ALU enables, and divisor-width flags removed late decode from wide mux paths and raised median Gowin Fmax to 76.909 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles, with the div\n…[1613 of 4685 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a16d8f79ab9423fe]…\nt successors advance while the load-use interlock held immediate consumers for MEM/WB forwarding, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles by 1.67% with the divider's prefix trial logic capping median Gowin Fmax at 89.170 MHz, below the 89.589 MHz needed for acceptance.\n33:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r12s1 (regression, +1.31%): Moving the load-use interlock into ID/EX removed replayed source fields from fetch FIFO pop and head promotion at unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles, with paths from the registered MEM/WB match to the ALU subtract result and from the EX/MEM result to the divider prefix capping median Gowin Fmax at 88.248 MHz, below the 91.11 MHz acceptance threshold.\n37:- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r13s2 (regression, -2.07%): Bypassing the load-use interlock for BEQ/BNE with registered rollback cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 3.78%, with replay-to-FIFO issue feedback and nearly tied EX forwarding paths into the ALU and divider capping median Gowin Fmax at 86.390 MHz against the 88.22 MHz needed to break even.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
