{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n --no-ignore 'hyp-20261120-002-r1s1|hyp-20261120-001-r1s0|703\\\\.14' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share a clocked radix-4 divider in EX\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline reaches 5.442 MHz, with a 156-cell worst path through forwarding and the EX ALU. The unregistered signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the leading explanation for that depth. A baseline RVFI trace with both bus stall models enabled recorded zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU retirements in the 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles between the markers. Both DIVU retirements occurred outside the measured interval, and the baseline run matched the reference CRCs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench baseline reaches 5.442 MHz, with a 156-cell worst path through forwarding and the EX ALU. The unregistered signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the leading explanation for that depth. A baseline RVFI trace with both bus stall models enabled recorded zero DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU retirements in the 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles between the markers. Both DIVU retirements occurred outside the measured interval, and the baseline run matched the reference CRCs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational divide and remainder operators with one blocking, unsigned radix-4 restoring divider inside EX. Capture magnitudes from the forwarded operands and precompute the divisor multiples D, 2D and 3D. Use six clocks to process a 36-bit zero-extended dividend at three radix-4 digits per clock. For each digit, form a 34-bit trial dividend from the partial remainder and the next two dividend bits. Use parallel 35-bit differences to select the greatest divisor multiple without borrow, including zero, and produce the next remainder and quotient digit. Finish with a registered sign correction and quotient or remainder selection. Signed division truncates toward zero and signed remainder follows the dividend sign. Return the RV32M-defined values on divisor zero and INT_MIN divided by -1.\\n\\nHold fetch and ID/EX while the divide is outstanding and let older instructions drain. \n…[7443 of 10515 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3bb57f078c2d1e0b]…\n Fmax | 5.442 MHz | 45.180 MHz |\\n| CoreMark iterations/second | 12.12 | 100.59 |\\n\\n`make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1` reported 43.715, 45.180, and 45.180 MHz for Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2. Option 0's worst path spans 49 cells through regfile read, redirect, and hazard logic, compared with the baseline's 156-cell worst path through the ALU. Fitness increased 730.2%. The stalled CoreMark trace recorded two DIVU retirements outside the markers and zero DIV or REM family retirements in the measured interval. Divide-heavy workloads pay the eight additional EX cycles per operation.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T02:51:04.309128Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (improvement, +729.95%): Replacing combinational `/` and `%` with a shared, blocking radix-4 divider removed the long EX ALU path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 45.180 MHz, with unchanged CoreMark cycles because no divide or remainder retired between the markers to incur the eight-cycle EX stall.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
