{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i -m 35 '4,?220,?145|13\\\\.14|fmax|critical.path|cycles:|canonical|accepted' .tmp/agent-logs/impl.hyp-20261120-003-r1s2.log\"", "aggregated_output": "14:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -c \\\"sed -n '1,260p' formal/run_all.sh && sed -n '1,265p' Makefile && cat cores/bench/core.yaml\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Run riscv-formal checks against the current $RTL_DIR/*.sv.\\n# Requires: formal/riscv-formal cloned (manual clone or `git submodule init`).\\n#\\n# Stages every $RTL_DIR/*.sv plus wrapper.sv + the chosen checks config under\\n# the riscv-formal tree, then runs sby -> bitwuzla via the framework's\\n# generated makefile. Tallies PASS/FAIL by inspecting each task's\\n# logfile.txt.\\n#\\n# Usage: bash formal/run_all.sh [checks-cfg-path]\\n#   default: formal/checks.cfg       (fast, ALTOPS, used by orchestrator)\\n#   deep   : formal/checks-deep.cfg  (no ALTOPS, proves M-ext arithmetic)\\n#\\n# Env vars (orchestrator-driven, override $1 and defaults):\\n#   WRAPPER     — path to wrapper SV (default formal/wrapper.sv)\\n#   CHECKS_CFG  — path to checks config (default formal/checks.cfg)\\n# When BOTH are unset, the script auto-detects nret from\\n# cores/$CORE_NAME/core.yaml: nret=1 routes to wrapper_si.sv +\\n# checks_si.cfg; nret=2 (or absent) uses the defaults. Agents\\n# invoking `bash formal/run_all.sh` directly therefore get the\\n# right wrapper without needing to know the env-var plumbing.\\nset -e\\n\\nif [ -z \\\"${RTL_DIR:-}\\\" ] || [ -z \\\"${CORE_NAME:-}\\\" ]; then\\n  echo \\\"ERROR: formal/run_all.sh requires RTL_DIR and CORE_NAME env vars.\\\" >&2\\n  echo \\\"  Example: RTL_DIR=cores/v1/rtl CORE_NAME=v1 bash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks.cfg\\\" >&2\\n  exit 2\\nfi\\n\\nSCRIPT_DIR=\\\"$(cd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")\\\" && pwd)\\\"\\nPROJECT_ROOT=\\\"$(cd \\\"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\\\" && pwd)\\\"\\nRISCV_FORMAL=\\\"$SCRIPT_DIR/riscv-formal\\\"\\n# Per-invocation work dir + log, unique among every process sharing this\\n# riscv-formal checkout (the K concurrent agents of a run share it through\\n# their worktrees' symlink). The name used to be \\\"$CORE_NAME-$$\\\", but an\\n# agent's sandboxed shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or\n…[76728 of 79800 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8e49c929d0ca40df]…\nd without both bus stalls. An alternating-JALR loop with stores at both targets also matched the ISS under both stalls for five seeds. The explicit `NRET=1` selects the existing single-channel C++ build.\\n- Stalled CoreMark finished in 4,220,145 marker-bracketed cycles, down 6.04% from 4,491,485, with all five canonical CRCs and the validation message.\\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 5.065, 5.546, and 5.546 MHz. Option 0 used 12,446 LUT4s and eight BSRAMs. The 5.546 MHz median yields 13.14 CoreMark iterations/sec, up 8.43% from 12.12.\\n\\n## Limitations\\n\\nDifferent PCs sharing an index can evict each other, and alternating outcomes or targets can repeatedly mispredict. A stale entry at a PC whose instruction has changed to a non-control instruction can continue to predict until overwritten. EX corrects each mismatch before the younger instruction can issue. The worst local Gowin paths still run through EX forwarding and the ALU into the data-memory path.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
