{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_34", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,235p' fpga/core_bench_si.sv && sed -n '40,60p' test/cosim/run_cosim.py && rg -n 'rvfi_rs1_addr|rvfi_rs2_addr' /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep2/formal/riscv-formal/checks/rvfi_reg_check.sv\"", "aggregated_output": "// fpga/core_bench_si.sv\n//\n// FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\n// core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\n// set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\n// synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n//\n// See core_bench.sv for the rationale on LFSR-driven imem, the dmem\n// model, the V2 stall generator, and why the LED observes only the\n// memory-side outputs (RVFI-only logic is pruned).\nmodule core_bench (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic led\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] lfsr;\n  always_ff @(posedge clock or posedge reset)\n    if (reset) lfsr <= 32'h1;\n    else       lfsr <= {lfsr[30:0], lfsr[31] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[1] ^ lfsr[0]};\n\n  logic [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n  logic [31:0] dmem_rdata;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_addr;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n  logic [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n  logic        dmem_ren;\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (dmem_wen[0]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][7:0]   <= dmem_wdata[7:0];\n    if (dmem_wen[1]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][15:8]  <= dmem_wdata[15:8];\n    if (dmem_wen[2]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][23:16] <= dmem_wdata[23:16];\n    if (dmem_wen[3]) dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]][31:24] <= dmem_wdata[31:24];\n  end\n  assign dmem_rdata = dmem[dmem_addr[12:2]];\n\n  logic [31:0] imem_addr;\n  // Single-channel RVFI port set (NRET=1 contract). Connected for\n  // elaboration only; nothing observes it, so synthesis prunes it.\n  logic        rvfi_valid_0;\n  logic [63:0] rvfi_order_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_insn_0, rvfi_pc_rdata_0, rvfi_pc_wdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_rd_wdata_0, rvfi_rs1_rdata_0, rvfi_rs2_rdata_0;\n  logic [31:0] rvfi_mem_addr_0, rvfi_mem_rdata_0, rvfi_mem_wdata_0;\n  logic [4:0]  rvfi_rs1_addr_0, rvfi_rs2_addr_0, rvfi_rd_addr_0;\n  logic [3:0]  rvfi_mem_rmask_0, rvfi_mem_wmask_0;\n  logic [1:0]  rvfi_mode_0, rvfi_ixl_0;\n  logic        rvfi_trap_0, rvfi_halt_0, rvfi_intr_0;\n\n  // V2: memory-ready signals come from the same stall sequence the\n  // CoreMark simulation uses, so stall-handling logic\n…[2461 of 5533 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5ab7bfcc33e7c4a0]…\n'insn', 'insn'),\n                       ('rd', 'rd_addr'), ('rd_wdata', 'rd_wdata'),\n                       ('rs1', 'rs1_addr'), ('rs1_rdata', 'rs1_rdata'),\n                       ('rs2', 'rs2_addr'), ('rs2_rdata', 'rs2_rdata'),\n                       ('trap', 'trap'), ('halt', 'halt'), ('intr', 'intr'),\n                       ('mode', 'mode'), ('ixl', 'ixl')]:\n            if ref.get(rf, 0) != sim.get(sf, 0):\n                return _mismatch(i, ref, rf, ref.get(rf, 0), sim.get(sf, 0))\n        # RVFI ALIGNED_MEM convention: mem_addr is word-aligned; byte offset is\n        # encoded in the shifted byte-lane mask. Reference ISS reports raw byte\n        # address + unshifted width mask (1 byte / 3 half / 15 word). Normalize.\n        byte_off   = ref.get('mem_addr', 0) & 3\n        ref_addr   = ref.get('mem_addr', 0) & ~3\n41:\t\t\t\tif (register_written && register_index == rvfi_rs1_addr[`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX*5 +: 5])\n43:\t\t\t\tif (register_written && register_index == rvfi_rs2_addr[`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX*5 +: 5])\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261120-001-r10s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; r=json.loads(sys.stdin.readline()); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"critical_path\\\"])' && ls bench/programs/*elf\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261120-001-r10s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; r=json.loads(sys.stdin.readline()); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"critical_path\\\"])' && ls bench/programs/*elf\"","aggregated_output":"# Width-aware adjacent load branch resolution\n\n- Fetch now evaluates the queued and fall-through load interlocks independently. The bus, replay RAM, and last fill each classify equality branches and source tags before source selection. Full-word comparisons against a distinct nonzero register and other load consumers retain the existing interlock.\n- Decode registers the producer-qualified forwarding, ALU operand, and ALU operation masks. EX checks alignment before advertising a load producer and captures the other operand's low half and extension check. MEM compares at most 16 load bits, uses the registered byte-lane zero result for x0 compares, and treats two matches to the load destination as equal. RVFI keeps both full operands.\n- EX captures both possible successor verdicts. An older MEM miss overrides a younger EX redirect and training, annuls younger EX effects, and inhibits M-unit requests. IF reuses the saved branch PCs and source tag, registers replay invalidation, and holds the correction through an instruction-bus stall. MEM releases the completed branch retirement on the following edge.\n- Decode captures the load-source matches along with the forwarding enables. EX computes ordinary branch ordering with parallel byte comparisons and uses registered ALU operation enables. The multiplier and divider retain their existing execution schedules.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed. All eight cocotb suites passed during development, and the ALU, fetch, and pipeline suites passed again on the delivered RTL. The directed tests cover subword signedness and extension mismatches, zero and identical-source comparisons, held and trapping loads, target alignment, stale replay, and recovery over younger stores and M operations.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` passed for all five ELFs after `make bench`, including selftest and three random-trace ELFs with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark reproduced 3,402,673 marked cycles with all canonical CRCs and no out-of-bounds accesses, a 3.88% cycle reduction from 3,540,112.\n- The unmodified single-channel formal suite passed 52 of 53 checks on the delivered RTL, including liveness, unique order, control-flow consistency, BEQ, and BNE. `reg_ch0` is still solving with no reported counterexample.\n- Gowin options 1 and 2 routed at 82.701 MHz. The matching results fix the median at 82.701 MHz if option 0 completes successfully. That corresponds to 243.05 CoreMark iterations/sec and falls below the 84.375 MHz acceptance threshold. The longest setup paths run from the queue write pointer through the oldest-PC check to recovery, and from the recovery direction to the replay RAM. Option 0 is still generating reports.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_if/pc_q_2_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.divide_s0', 'slack_ns': -7.212, 'arrival_ns': 17.506, 'logic_cells': 19, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_id/u_decoder', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_id/u_decoder', 'cpu/u_id']}\nls: cannot access 'bench/programs/*elf': No such file or directory\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
