{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d['id'] in ('hyp-20261120-003-r12s2','hyp-20261120-001-r13s0','hyp-20261120-002-r12s1'):\n  print(d['id']); print(d['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r12s1\n# Registered operand interlocks\n\nID/EX can now accept an instruction immediately behind a load and registers\nthe nonzero source matches for both older pipeline entries. MEM qualifies\nthe older match with its alignment-checked write intent as the producer\nenters MEM/WB. EX uses that match and gives a write-enabled EX/MEM hit\npriority. Decode readiness depends on the issue slot, the M unit, and data\nbus backpressure, which removes the incoming instruction fields from the\nfetch queue's pop and head promotion path.\n\nA waiting slot issues no memory operation, predictor update, or RVFI\nretirement and cannot launch the M unit. The slot and its selects hold\nduring data bus stalls. As the load enters MEM/WB, ID/EX saves any matched\ndeparting WB operand, clears the EX/MEM matches, and selects the load from\nMEM/WB for the affected operands only if it can write. A misaligned load\ntraps and the consumer sees the preserved architectural value.\n\nDirected pipeline tests check the one-cycle load interlock, a departing WB\noperand on either source port, a misaligned load in either forwarding\nposition, and a stalled load feeding a taken branch or DIV. The branch\ntest checks the exact bus handshake sequence. The DIV test checks that\nfetch can fill all four FIFO entries while the dependent DIV is already\nin ID/EX, then checks DIV retirement spacing and captured operands.\n\n## Recovery and execute timing\n\nEX computes both successor mismatches against the full-width oldest\nunissued PC. IF registers them alongside the actual direction and\nselects the redirect from those registers. The corrected fetch still\nwaits for instruction-bus readiness, and the registered recovery state\nsquashes the speculative ID/EX slot. Branch direction uses parallel byte\ncomparisons. Branch direction also stays off the EX/MEM register-write\nand memory-enable paths.\n\nID registers the base ALU's one-hot result controls. EX combines the PC\nand immediate operand selects with the forwarding masks. The multiply\nand divide units retain their requ\n…[2137 of 5209 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f0ca24545b507878]…\nThe three local Gowin placements measured 85.339, 89.691, and 89.691 MHz with 5,659 LUT4s and 971 registers, 32 more than the champion. The 89.691 MHz median gives 257.06 CoreMark iterations per second and falls short of the locally reproduced 91.689 MHz champion result and the 95.91 MHz promotion threshold. The median placement's forwarding path from ID/EX through the ALU reaches the integer result register at 16.444 ns. The champion's worst path reached the EX/MEM result register at 16.201 ns.\n\nThe direct split had the highest median of the tested split variants. Folding the integer mask into opcode selection measured 88.013 MHz, and explicitly grouping the ALU's one-hot operation terms measured 88.380 MHz. A parallel three-input MEM writeback mux measured 88.720 MHz. Folding reset into the EX result masks measured 79.745 MHz.\n\nThe stock single-channel ALTOPS run is incomplete. Fifty-two of 53 checks passed. `reg_ch0` was still solving step 20 after more than 26 minutes, with no verdict or counterexample.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
