{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_26", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d['round_id']>=13:\n  print(d['id'],d['title'])\n  for l in d['implementation_notes'].splitlines():\n   if any(term in l for term in ('cycle','branch','fit','predicate','ADD','forward','mispredict')):\n    print(l)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r13s0 Move integer result selection after the EX/MEM register\nEX/MEM captures the integer ALU value and the combined PC+4, AUIPC, multiply, and divide value in two zero-masked fields. Both fields use the bundle's existing reset and dmem hold. EX forwards each field as a parallel term under the same EX/MEM hit for rs1, rs2, and the immediate-aware ALU B mux. MEM ORs the two fields for the non-load input to the existing MEM/WB writeback mux.\nThe implementation follows the proposed datapath and uses the existing `alu_result` field for the integer lane. The directed pipeline tests cover back-to-back dependences on integer, PC-relative, link, multiply, and divide producers. They check ALU B immediate selection when the immediate aliases a forwarded register. They also check direct store-data forwarding and forwarding through MEM/WB during a dmem hold for both lanes.\nVerilator lint and all 100 cocotb tests passed. Selftest and all three random programs matched the ISS with zero wait states and with both bus stalls enabled. Stalled CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs in 3,489,128 marked cycles.\nThe three local Gowin placements measured 85.339, 89.691, and 89.691 MHz with 5,659 LUT4s and 971 registers, 32 more than the champion. The 89.691 MHz median gives 257.06 CoreMark iterations per second and falls short of the locally reproduced 91.689 MHz champion result and the 95.91 MHz promotion threshold. The median placement's forwarding path from ID/EX through the ALU reaches the integer result register at 16.444 ns. The champion's worst path reached the EX/MEM result register at 16.201 ns.\nhyp-20261120-002-r13s1 Complete adjacent MUL consumers in the memory stage\nID records the matching nonzero source tags and selects legal register ADD or store-data consumers. A paired ADD completes in MEM using the registered product and the other forwarded operand. A double match produces the product shifted left by one bit. A dependent successor waits for the ADD to enter MEM/WB. A paired store uses the product for data\n…[7975 of 11047 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3c04887e3aaa0735]…\nrs trigger the same annulment and retry. Accepted consumers report the preceding load’s real writeback value on their affected RVFI operands.\n- Validation gates backend side effects immediately. Fetch handles the retry on the following cycle, holds the consumer PC through instruction-bus stalls, and retains replay entries. Failed instructions cannot train either predictor.\n- Fetch computes the load-use checks for the buffered and incoming words in parallel. ID registers the ordinary forwarding masks alongside the raw EX/MEM matches. Only the separate branch comparison and pointer address use the predicted data.\n- Stalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,303,482 marked cycles, down 5.32% from 3,489,128. At the measured median clock, throughput was 268.51 iterations per second.\n- Gowin placements reached 81.752, 88.703, and 88.703 MHz with seven block RAMs. Median Fmax was 88.703 MHz, below the 90.80 MHz promotion threshold. The median placements were limited by the EX result forwarding path into the ALU.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
