{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_41", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -i 'MULT36X36|MULTADDALU|DSP' /opt/gowin/IDE/simlib/gw2ar -l | head -25 && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d['id'] in ('hyp-20261120-001-r3s0','hyp-20261120-001-r12s0'):\n  print(d['id'], str(d['hypothesis'])[:4700]); print(d['implementation_notes'][:3400])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "rg: /opt/gowin/IDE/simlib/gw2ar: IO error for operation on /opt/gowin/IDE/simlib/gw2ar: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nhyp-20261120-001-r3s0 {'motivation': 'With redirect recovery registered, the champion reaches 49.767 MHz and\\n117.93 CoreMark iterations per second. The median Gowin placement reports\\na 25.388 ns arrival from the register-file block RAM through the unsigned\\nmultiply DSP and six result-selection LUTs to a data-memory block RAM.\\nThe four multiply operations currently share the same EX result path as\\ninteger operations and effective addresses. Registering the DSP output\\nseparately can cut that path while keeping integer and memory latency.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Feed one signed 33-by-33 multiply directly from the two forwarded EX\\noperands. Extend rs1 with its sign for MULH and MULHSU, and extend rs2\\nwith its sign for MULH. Zero-extend each operand in the other cases.\\nCapture the low 64 product bits in the EX/MEM register along with a valid\\nmultiply flag and the requested result half. The low 32 bits implement\\nMUL for every signedness. The upper 32 bits implement MULH, MULHSU or\\nMULHU with the corresponding operand extension. Infer the native Gowin\\nDSP from the signed multiply expression. Select the requested half in MEM\\nand capture it in MEM/WB for normal writeback, forwarding and retirement.\\nThe generic EX result mux handles integer and address results as well as\\nlink and divider results.\\n\\nReuse the existing one-cycle late-result interlock for a following\\ninstruction whose source field matches a nonzero multiply destination.\\nUse the existing EX validity, fetch readiness and backend hold conditions\\nfor the interlock. The source-field comparison remains conservative for\\nimmediate encodings. Independent multiplies can still issue on consecutive\\ncycles. Exclude pending multiply results from EX/MEM forwarding, and hold\\nthe product and its instruction metadata with the existing EX/MEM enable.\\nOnly an advancing, nonsquashed multiply can set the pending flag. Keep the\\nfour orig\n…[8785 of 11857 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c9d9717a4709741a]…\non\n\n- Verilator lint passed in real and ALTOPS builds. All 99 cocotb tests across eight suites passed. The arithmetic tests cover exact low and high products, consecutive capture edges, and the high-half handshake. Pipeline tests cover dependent and independent MULs, x0, mixed multiply and divide operations, both stages' trap gating, and launch suppression during dmem holds and recovery.\n- ISS cosimulation matched selftest and all three random programs with zero-wait and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs in 3,430,915 marked cycles, 1.67% fewer than the starting core.\n- Of the 53 stock ALTOPS checks, 52 completed and passed. The solver remained on `reg_ch0` at depth 20, with no counterexample.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 measured 89.077, 89.170 and 89.170 MHz. At the median, CoreMark scored 259.90 iterations per second, 4.11% above the starting core. The median falls 0.419 MHz below the 89.589 MHz acceptance threshold. Its worst path crosses the divider's prefix trial logic.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
