{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n 'micro_opt|hyp-20261120-003-r2s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (improvement, +729.95%): Replacing combinational `/` and `%` with a shared, blocking radix-4 divider removed the long EX ALU path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 45.180 MHz, with unchanged CoreMark cycles because no divide or remainder retired between the markers to incur the eight-cycle EX stall.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.42%): Avoiding redirects with a 64-entry tagged next-PC table cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.04%, with the combinational EX divider still limiting median Gowin Fmax to 5.546 MHz and leaving throughput well behind the round winner, whose clocked divider raised Fmax to 45.180 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.23%): Driving fetch directly from the registered EX/MEM redirect target while invalidating the younger EX slot preserved CoreMark's cycle count and removed the EX-to-IF-to-ID redirect path from Gowin's worst paths, leaving the register-file-to-data-memory path through the EX ALU to cap median Fmax at 47.542 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r2s1 (regression, +13.90%): A one-entry fall-through fetch buffer cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 3.07% by saving ready instructions during load-use and backend holds for later instruction-bus stalls, and using the registered full bit to select the decode payload while applying redirects to ID/EX validity removed the wide redirect path from Gowin's worst-path reports as median Fmax rose from 45.180 to 49.878 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (improvement, +17.24%): Registering the next-PC mismatch and capturing the resolved target independently cuts the EX-to-ID redirect path, while annulling the younger EX slot as the target enters ID/EX keeps misses to one bubble, letting the tagged successor predictor cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.04% as median Gowin Fmax rises from 45.180 to 49.767 MHz.\n1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-74169ac\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 12.12, \"delta_pct\": 0.0,\n…[29836 of 32908 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0a5e101dcec39d99]…\nn- ISS co-simulation passed selftest and all three random traces with and without both bus stalls. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC check with 4,220,145 marker-bracketed cycles under both stalls.\\n- `make fpga TARGET=bench` scored 117.93 iterations/sec, above the 105.22 threshold. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 45.530, 49.767, and 49.767 MHz. Option 0 used 3,714 LUT4s and its longest reported path crossed the ALU to dmem.\\n\\nNo correctness issues surfaced in the local checks. The tests ran in a temporary staging directory.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T03:14:22.524460Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (improvement, +17.24%): Registering the next-PC mismatch and capturing the resolved target independently cuts the EX-to-ID redirect path, while annulling the younger EX slot as the target enters ID/EX keeps misses to one bubble, letting the tagged successor predictor cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.04% as median Gowin Fmax rises from 45.180 to 49.767 MHz.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
