er LUT RAM. Preserve same-cycle WB write-through\\non either read port and hardwire x0. This lets the ID/EX operand flops\\nlaunch EX directly and removes the register-file BRAM clock-to-out delay.\\n\\nIn ID, compare each source address against the nonzero destination\\nof the current ID/EX and EX/MEM instructions. Register the four match\\nbits with the same hold and reset behavior as the ID/EX payload.\\nIn EX, qualify these matches with the actual registered EX/MEM and\\nMEM/WB forwarding write enables, which already account for bubbles\\nand traps. Select EX/MEM first, then MEM/WB, then the latched register\\nvalue with mutually exclusive masks and a bitwise OR. A dmem hold\\npreserves the match bits and b\n…[28610 of 31682 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:078756695aacb694]…\nider tests.\n- The single-channel co-simulation passed for selftest and the three random traces with and without bus stalls. CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs with both stalls enabled in 4,313,521 marked cycles.\n- The preprocessed ALTOPS RTL matches the champion after stripping whitespace and comments. A Yosys SAT proof with a reset base case and temporal induction verified output equality under arbitrary requests and result backpressure.\n- Standalone SAT checks proved `trial = divisor × digit + remainder` and `remainder < divisor` for every legal trial in each of the four digit configurations.\n- Local `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL, including `reg_ch0`.\n- Gowin measured 67.669, 72.226, and 72.226 MHz, for a median of 72.226 MHz and 167.44 CoreMark iterations per second, 9.19% above the champion. The mapped design uses 4,295 LUT4s and 766 registers. The LUT4 count is 68 above the scratch result. The worst setup paths cross EX forwarding into the predictor.\n \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
