{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261120-002-r5s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "Split the blocking divider into two remainder datapaths selected by the\ncaptured unsigned divisor magnitude. Share the 32-bit dividend/quotient\nshift register, divisor multiples, phase counter and result interface.\nAt capture, choose the narrow path for divisor magnitudes below 4096\nand the wide path otherwise.\n\nFor a nonzero narrow divisor, the partial remainder is strictly less\nthan the divisor and fits in 12 bits. On the first work edge, consume\nthe top two dividend bits with the existing small prefix lookup.\nOn each of the next six edges, consume five bits. Use a radix-4 step\nover a 14-bit trial followed by a radix-8 step over a 15-bit trial.\nEach step subtracts candidate multiples in parallel and uses adjacent\nborrow flags to select the largest fitting multiple and its remainder.\nAppend the two quotient bits followed by the three quotient bits.\n\nFor a wide divisor, the top 12 dividend bits are smaller than the\ndivisor, so the top 12 quotient bits are zero. On the first work edge,\nseed the remainder by processing the top 14 dividend bits in one\nradix-4 step. Use three 16-bit candidate multiples for divisors below\n16384. At or above 16384 the prefix already fits as the remainder and\nthe two quotient bits are zero. Append the 12 known-zero bits and the\ntwo computed bits to the remaining dividend in the shift register.\nOn the next six edges, consume the remaining 18 bits with one\nfull-width radix-8 step per edge with a 35-bit trial. Register the\nshared 35-bit odd multiples 3D, 5D and 7D on the prefix edge and\nderive the even multiples by shifts.\n\nSelect the completed quotient or the chosen remainder from held\nregisters and apply sign correction during the result-valid cycle.\nFor a zero divisor, capture the original dividend for REM or REMU\nand all ones for DIV or DIVU in the quotient register. Freeze that\nvalue until the same scheduled completion. Signed INT_MIN divided\nby -1 still returns INT_MIN with zero remainder.\nKeep the ALTOPS formulas and result staging on the common phase\ncounter. Capture the substitute\n…[2593 of 5665 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8dec98876e322da3]…\nivider tests.\n- The single-channel co-simulation passed for selftest and the three random traces with and without bus stalls. CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs with both stalls enabled in 4,313,521 marked cycles.\n- The preprocessed ALTOPS RTL matches the champion after stripping whitespace and comments. A Yosys SAT proof with a reset base case and temporal induction verified output equality under arbitrary requests and result backpressure.\n- Standalone SAT checks proved `trial = divisor × digit + remainder` and `remainder < divisor` for every legal trial in each of the four digit configurations.\n- Local `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL, including `reg_ch0`.\n- Gowin measured 67.669, 72.226, and 72.226 MHz, for a median of 72.226 MHz and 167.44 CoreMark iterations per second, 9.19% above the champion. The mapped design uses 4,295 LUT4s and 766 registers. The LUT4 count is 68 above the scratch result. The worst setup paths cross EX forwarding into the predictor.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
