{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261120-003-r6s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"critical_path\\\"])'; sed -n '1,150p' Makefile; ls -la /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh; date -u\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261120-003-r6s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(r[\\\"critical_path\\\"])'; sed -n '1,150p' Makefile; ls -la /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh; date -u\"", "aggregated_output": "# Bench implementation notes\n\n## RTL\n\nFetch uses a 128-entry target table indexed by `PC[8:2] ^ PC[15:9]`. Each entry stores the full `PC[31:9]` tag, a conditional bit, and the target's word offset in the source page. A staged write updates the target on legal page-local taken transfers and invalidates the slot on taken cross-page transfers. Branches train a saturating two-bit counter on both outcomes, starting at weakly untaken. Fetch reads a copy of the high counter bit so both predictor reads infer distributed RAM. The staged tag carries the source page, and the separate recovery address register also supplies target write data.\n\nA registered decode head and 15 usable slots in a 16-slot RAM ring let fetch advance during backend holds. The head's saved successor advances the decode PC on each dequeue, even when the head empties. EX checks that register for all advancing instructions. A ready correction bypasses the head when ID/EX can advance, or fills the head during a data hold. Recovery clears the held younger ID/EX valid bit before the wrong-path instruction can launch or issue a memory request.\n\nThe fetch increment computes the page offset and upper-page increment in parallel and carries into the page on sequential overflow. This and the separate direction lookup improved Gowin timing and preserved the same instruction-fetch behavior.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all seven cocotb runners passed. Directed pipeline tests cover the counter in both directions, full tags at a shared page offset, cross-page invalidation, full-width fall-through and alignment traps, a full ring during a data hold, stale predictions, and a 10-cycle redirect-to-division retirement gap.\n- The ISS comparison passed for selftest and all three random programs in both zero-wait and dual-bus stall modes. CoreMark passed CRC validation with both buses stalled and completed 10 iterations in 3,994,772 marked cycles.\n- `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` reported 70.013, 73.253 and 73.253 MHz for Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2. The\n…[6027 of 9099 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1431753ad99fb9a5]…\nal gate uses fast `make formal`.\n# See formal/checks.cfg comments for the full ALTOPS scope explanation.\nformal-deep:\n\tbash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks-deep.cfg\n\nbench:\n\t$(MAKE) -f bench/programs/Makefile all\n\n# FPGA timing and area come from the vendor flow, Gowin EDA\n# (tools/eval/gowin.py); V2 does not synthesize with Yosys.\nfpga: cosim-build bench/programs/coremark.elf\n\tpython3 -m tools.eval.fpga . $(TARGET)\n\ntiming:\n\tpython3 -m tools.eval.gowin $(TARGET)\n\nbench/programs/coremark.elf: bench/programs/Makefile bench/programs/crt0.S \\\n                              $(wildcard bench/programs/coremark/*.c) \\\n                              $(wildcard bench/programs/coremark/*.h) \\\n                              $(wildcard bench/programs/coremark/baremetal/*.c) \\\n                              $(wildcard bench/programs/coremark/baremetal/*.h)\n\t$(MAKE) -f bench/programs/Makefile bench/programs/coremark.elf\n\nnext:\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nogroup 24968 Jul 10  2025 /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\nFri Nov 20 06:38:40 UTC 2026\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
