{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nfor row in rows[-5:]:\n print(row['id'], row['critical_path'], '\\\\n', row['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis',''), '\\\\n',row.get('implementation_notes','')[:3000], '\\\\n')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r6s1 {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_val_8_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/recovering_s1', 'slack_ns': -8.32, 'arrival_ns': 18.614, 'logic_cells': 33, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_ex']} \n Put a four-entry circular fall-through FIFO between instruction memory\nand decode. Each entry holds the full PC and instruction word in\nasynchronous-read, synchronous-write distributed RAM. Track read and\nwrite pointers with wrap bits, resetting only the queue state. Advance\nthe fetch PC only when a ready response can bypass to decode or enter\nthe queue. A full queue can pop and refill on the same edge. Decode\nalways uses the oldest buffered entry when one exists and can issue\nit while imem is stalled. Feed the existing load-use and backend\ninterlocks with this selected word and its availability.\n\nVerify each advancing EX instruction against the PC of the oldest\nunissued instruction. That is the FIFO head when occupied and the\ncurrent fetch address when empty, even if imem is waiting. Enqueuing\ninto an empty queue during an EX hold preserves this PC because the\nnew head has the prior fetch address. This removes the need to\npropagate a second PC through each FIFO entry and ID/EX.\n\nRegister the two recovery candidates separately in IF. One is PC+4.\nThe other is the relative target or the JALR sum with bit zero cleared,\nselected by decoded instruction type. Register the resolved choice\nalongside them and select the recovery PC after the edge. Keep\nfull-width comparison and the existing alignment and trap behavior\nin EX. Use the registered candidates, choice and paired training PC\nfor a one-edge-deferred table write. Retain the tagged last-successor\npolicy, including training conditional fall-through.\n\nOn a valid EX redirect, reset both FIFO pointers regardless of\nconcurrent push or pop and annul the younger ID/EX slot. Track issuing\nthe target fetch separately from issuing that corrected instruction\ninto ID/EX. Fetch can resume from the corrected stream as soon as the\ntarget response is bypassed or buffere\n…[24940 of 28012 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9677474fa55b7cf7]…\nStalled CoreMark produced all five canonical CRCs and printed `Correct operation validated.` The explicit `NRET=1` selects the bench core's existing RVFI width.\n- Stalled CoreMark took 3,758,572 marked cycles, down 6.39% from 4,015,275.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 79.030, 79.292, and 79.292 MHz. The 79.292 MHz median yields 210.96 CoreMark iterations/s, up 9.39% from the 77.436 MHz champion at its marked cycle count.\n- The replay storage grew option 0 to 7,539 logic cells and 1,104 registers. EX recovery is on the longest path, with the divider close on options 1 and 2.\n- Local formal was incomplete within the wall-clock budget. The unchanged `formal/run_all.sh` gate reported 14 of 53 checks passing on the selected RTL, including illegal-instruction trapping, order uniqueness, liveness, and both PC checks. Zero failures or counterexamples were reported. Bitwuzla spent substantially longer unrolling the registered queue discard design, and the register consistency check was still solving.\n \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
