{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [print(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\",x.get(\\\"critical_path\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\") for x in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-74169ac Baseline retest for cores/bench \n  \n {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -189.542, 'arrival_ns': 199.837, 'logic_cells': 156, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} \n\nhyp-20261120-001-r1s0 Share a clocked radix-4 divider in EX \n Replace the combinational divide and remainder operators with one blocking, unsigned radix-4 restoring divider inside EX. Capture magnitudes from the forwarded operands and precompute the divisor multiples D, 2D and 3D. Use six clocks to process a 36-bit zero-extended dividend at three radix-4 digits per clock. For each digit, form a 34-bit trial dividend from the partial remainder and the next two dividend bits. Use parallel 35-bit differences to select the greatest divisor multiple without borrow, including zero, and produce the next remainder and quotient digit. Finish with a registered sign correction and quotient or remainder selection. Signed division truncates toward zero and signed remainder follows the dividend sign. Return the RV32M-defined values on divisor zero and INT_MIN divided by -1.\n\nHold fetch and ID/EX while the divide is outstanding and let older instructions drain. When EX/MEM can advance before the result is ready, feed it an invalid bubble with writeback and memory enables clear. Start only when the older EX/MEM instruction can advance, then hold the result until EX/MEM accepts it once. Keep the completed result on the normal EX/MEM forwarding path and preserve regular ALU and multiply latency. In ALTOPS builds, capture the existing substitute result from the original operands and use the same completion timing so the gate exercises stalls and forwarding. Target EX/MEM transfer eight cycles after request capture with ready asserted. This leaves one cycle for a preceding redirect or load-use bubble within the formal wrapper's ten-cycle next-retirement window.\n\nThe prediction is a shorter Gowin worst path and higher median Fmax, with zero direct divide wait cycle\n…[17991 of 21063 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1e8d1d5981a4a8a5]…\ns residual, and\nshare the 32-bit dividend/quotient shift register.\n\nPreserve signed correction, INT_MIN divided by -1, the eight-edge\nrequest-to-transfer schedule, result stability under backpressure,\nand the existing ALTOPS formulas and timing. The scratch build\nreached 79.972, 85.362 and 85.362 MHz in Gowin, with 7,487 logic\ncells on option 0. Stalled CoreMark validated its CRCs in 3,758,572\nmarked cycles, yielding 227.11 iterations/sec at the median, about\n7.7% above the champion. The scratch divider passed the cocotb suite\nwith added signed and unsigned lane-boundary cases. Selftest and all\nthree random programs matched the ISS with and without stalls. The\nALTOPS-preprocessed divider is identical to the champion's. Re-run\nall three placements and the unmodified correctness gates after\nimplementation.\n \n {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.ctrl.reg_write_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/target_pending_q_s1', 'slack_ns': -7.613, 'arrival_ns': 17.907, 'logic_cells': 33, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_if']} \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
