{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor x in map(json.loads,Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()):\n if '-r8' not in x['id'] and '-r7' not in x['id']: continue\n print(x['id'],x['outcome'],x['fitness'],x['fmax_mhz'],x['cycles']); print('critical',x['critical_path']); print('perturbations',str(x['perturbations'])[:3200]); print('notes',str(x['implementation_notes'])[-4500:]); print()\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r7s0 regression 203.83 81.844 4015275\ncritical {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_4_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/read_ptr_q_0_s2', 'slack_ns': -7.064, 'arrival_ns': 17.358, 'logic_cells': 37, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_if']}\nperturbations [[0, 82.894, 14], [1, 81.844, 15], [2, 81.844, 15]]\nnotes # Bench RTL implementation\n\nID/EX now captures the one-hot raw forwarding masks, the ALU's PC and immediate operand masks, and the integer result enables. EX qualifies its producer tag with the live slot's write intent and JAL/JALR alignment. MEM qualifies its tag after checking load alignment. The younger EX producer has priority. The masks hold with ID/EX during stalls, and the MEM/WB payload remains available during a data bus hold.\n\nEX shares the one-hot integer datapath with the standalone encoded ALU and ORs the enabled integer result with the link and M results. JALR uses the forwarded address sum for alignment. JAL and branches use immediate bit 1 because fetch PCs are word-aligned and relative displacements are even. EX uses the full 32-bit successor comparison to redirect on the existing edge, and MEM/WB provides single-channel retirement.\n\nThe divider captures the first nibble's quotient and remainder and the byte cutoff at launch. It computes the top dividend digit from the raw sign and the low-28-bit zero test, in parallel with the lower magnitude bits. The signed divisor cutoff checks exclude exact magnitudes 16 and 256. The first work edge consumes the second nibble, with the same eight-edge result transfer and ALTOPS formulas.\n\nThe one-hot datapath lives alongside the encoded ALU in `alu.sv`, so existing standalone source lists still work. The final timing pass moved the add and subtract terms to the end of the result OR, simplified EX forwarding qualification using the decoder's write intent, and packed the first quotient nibble into the dividend/quotient register above the unconsumed magnitude bits. The first remainder nibble has its own register.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Ve\n…[12845 of 15917 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:df043c53b8a3dd06]…\nduced all five canonical CRCs and printed `Correct operation validated.`\n- Yosys proved fetch equivalence to the champion with `imem_ready` high and proved that the new divider predicates equal the original magnitude tests for every 32-bit divisor in both signedness modes.\n- The full `formal/run_all.sh` suite was incomplete locally. The earlier ready-bus-equivalent build completed 10 of 53 checks, including liveness, forward and backward PC continuity, uniqueness, illegal instructions, causal ordering, and retirement cover. The selected RTL was also submitted to the unchanged suite. Neither run produced a counterexample, so no formal fix retries were needed.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 76.388, 78.390, and 78.390 MHz. Option 0 used 5,917 logic cells, 1,183 registers, and six block RAMs. The median and 3,528,765 marked cycles yield 222.15 iterations/s, 5.30% above the champion's 210.96 iterations/s. The median is 0.523 MHz above the required clock for a 4.6% improvement at this cycle count.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
