{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r10s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-002-r10s1\ntitle: Register the replay response before operand read\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion delivers 237.06 CoreMark iter/sec at 83.921 MHz over\n  3,540,112 marked cycles. Its longest reported path enters replay RAM\n  through the recovery PC mux and tagged successor table. Round 9's\n  registered recovery address moved the median placement limit to replay\n  collision forwarding through the register-file read, reaching 86.171 MHz.\n  An elastic boundary between replay and operand read can cut that path\n  while retaining immediate issue of a ready corrected bus response.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split the fetch front end into request and issue stages. A bus stall with\n  an exact-tag replay hit accepts the current PC and resolved replay word\n  into a registered issue queue, including when that queue is empty. There\n  is no combinational path from a replay response into decode. Use the\n  registered head PC and word for decode and EX successor validation.\n  Keep an empty-queue bypass for a ready external response, including the\n  first ready corrected response. The oldest queued word always issues\n  first. Fetch can accept one new word each cycle when the queue has room.\n  When the queue is empty, EX still compares against the current request\n  PC, including the cycle in which a stalled request enqueues from replay.\n\n  Capture the selected full resolved PC at the EX-to-fetch boundary as in\n  round 9. Use it for both the held correction request and deferred\n  successor training. Keep the normal tagged next-PC lookup and launch\n  the synchronous replay read for the same next PC that fetch installs.\n  Bind the RAM output, full tag and read-during-write metadata to that\n  request throughout a hold. Resolve collision forwarding before the\n  issue queue edge. Keep RAM write admission independent of the decode\n  ready and redirect verdict paths. A registered correction discards the\n  queue and masks stale replay, and FENCE.I invalidates replay before any\n  post-fence word can be consumed. Retain the reset and invalidation scrub.\n\n  This preserves the one-cycle ready-bus redirect bubble and the ALTOPS\n  liveness schedule. An empty-queue replay hit costs one cycle before its\n  word can issue, so measure marked cycles as well as timing. At a 2%\n  cycle increase the median needs to exceed 89.54 MHz to clear the 4.6%\n  acceptance margin. A 94 MHz median at that cycle count would deliver\n  about 260 CoreMark iter/sec. Check the Gowin paths for both the replay\n  RAM to queue stage and the queue to register-file read stage.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Register the resolved recovery address and route accepted replay\n      responses through the issue queue. Retain direct issue of a ready\n      external word when no older buffered word exists. Preserve exact\n      PC tags, collision handling, and redirect and scrub priorities.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Check the registered replay response under an empty queue, a held\n      decode, and a following ready response. Exercise same-index fills,\n      queue discard, a ready redirected bypass, and FENCE.I refetch.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check successor continuity and exact-once in-order retirement for\n      alternating replay and ready-bus responses, including a corrected\n      DIV and wrong-path memory operations.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
