{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'r3s0|r3s1|r8s1|r9s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; keys=\\\"id title motivation hypothesis implementation_notes seeds perturbations\\\".split(); [print(*[j.get(k) for k in keys],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\",end=\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\") for j in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r3s0\nRegister a shared DSP multiplier product before result selection\nWith redirect recovery registered, the champion reaches 49.767 MHz and\n117.93 CoreMark iterations per second. The median Gowin placement reports\na 25.388 ns arrival from the register-file block RAM through the unsigned\nmultiply DSP and six result-selection LUTs to a data-memory block RAM.\nThe four multiply operations currently share the same EX result path as\ninteger operations and effective addresses. Registering the DSP output\nseparately can cut that path while keeping integer and memory latency.\n\n{'motivation': 'With redirect recovery registered, the champion reaches 49.767 MHz and\\n117.93 CoreMark iterations per second. The median Gowin placement reports\\na 25.388 ns arrival from the register-file block RAM through the unsigned\\nmultiply DSP and six result-selection LUTs to a data-memory block RAM.\\nThe four multiply operations currently share the same EX result path as\\ninteger operations and effective addresses. Registering the DSP output\\nseparately can cut that path while keeping integer and memory latency.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Feed one signed 33-by-33 multiply directly from the two forwarded EX\\noperands. Extend rs1 with its sign for MULH and MULHSU, and extend rs2\\nwith its sign for MULH. Zero-extend each operand in the other cases.\\nCapture the low 64 product bits in the EX/MEM register along with a valid\\nmultiply flag and the requested result half. The low 32 bits implement\\nMUL for every signedness. The upper 32 bits implement MULH, MULHSU or\\nMULHU with the corresponding operand extension. Infer the native Gowin\\nDSP from the signed multiply expression. Select the requested half in MEM\\nand capture it in MEM/WB for normal writeback, forwarding and retirement.\\nThe generic EX result mux handles integer and address results as well as\\nlink and divider results.\\n\\nReuse the existing one-cycle late-result interlock for a following\\ninstruction whose source field matches a nonzero multiply destination.\\nUse the existing\n…[17983 of 21055 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bbf3efdafa998397]…\n.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All eight cocotb runners passed.\n- Co-simulation passed for selftest and three random-trace ELFs with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed all five canonical CRC checks and reported 3,540,112 marked cycles.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 75.051, 86.171, and 86.171 MHz. The median gives 243.41 CoreMark iterations/sec, 2.68% above the cited champion and below the 87.783 MHz acceptance threshold. Placement option 0 used 5,369 logic cells, 1,047 registers, and six block RAMs. Options 1 and 2 were limited by replay collision forwarding through instruction delivery and register-file reads into ID.\n- Local single-channel ALTOPS formal was incomplete within the implementation window. The one run passed 52 of 53 checks. Bitwuzla was still solving step 20 of `reg_ch0` and produced no counterexample. There were no formal-driven RTL fixes or retries.\n\n[75.051, 86.171, 86.171]\n[[0, 75.051, 12], [1, 86.171, 12], [2, 86.171, 12]]\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
