{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'r9s0|r9s1|r10s0|r10s1|r10s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [(print(j[\\\"id\\\"],j[\\\"title\\\"],j[\\\"motivation\\\"],j[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\"),print()) for j in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'r9s0|r9s1|r10s0|r10s1|r10s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [(print(j[\\\"id\\\"],j[\\\"title\\\"],j[\\\"motivation\\\"],j[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\"),print()) for j in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r9s0\nResolve load-dependent equality branches from registered load data\nThe champion retired 135,320 BEQ/BNE instructions that immediately\nconsumed a load in the 3,540,112-cycle CoreMark interval. A scratch\ncombinational bypass passed the canonical CRCs in 3,406,310 cycles,\nbut reached only 58.434 MHz in Gowin placement option 1 because the\ndata memory path extended through EX into fetch recovery. Resolving\nthis branch in MEM can use the already registered load result while\neliminating the issue bubble.\n\n# Load-dependent equality branches\n\n- Aligned BEQ and BNE use a load-to-branch bypass. Decode still interlocks other load users. EX qualifies register producers using the effective address alignment and records the forwarding selects. A trapping load leaves the branch free to use the older writer.\n- EX checks both full-width successor PCs and incorporates BEQ/BNE polarity into the saved match bits. MEM selects the result using a dedicated register for the width- and sign-extended load value at the MEM/WB boundary. An older miss suppresses younger memory and RV32M requests and takes priority in fetch recovery. Late branches train the predictor on misses, leaving the port available for EX when a late prediction is correct.\n- Decode registers the one-hot forwarding and ALU operand masks. Fetch reuses the late branch's already captured successor candidates for recovery. The MEM match result drives recovery control without a path from the dmem response through the EX forwarding mux.\n- The first formal run found a bounded-liveness counterexample when a late miss targeted a divide. The branch now completes retirement while IF presents the registered recovery address. The corrected path issues on the same edge as before, and the divider keeps its existing schedule. Directed tests cover this sequence, predictor arbitration, held subword loads, both branch polarities, both sources matching, trapping loads, and wrong-path stores and RV32M requests.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- Verilator lint passed.\n- All eight \n…[10575 of 13647 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a8c27ce1c1706e69]…\n, plus the stalled CoreMark CRC check. All eight cocotb suites passed on this RTL before the final two predictor cases were added. The expanded suite passed on a later timing trial, and its rerun on the selected RTL was still in progress at handoff.\n- Stalled CoreMark completed ten iterations in 3,402,673 marked cycles. The canonical CRCs and validation message matched, and the simulator reported no out-of-bounds accesses.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 82.478, 79.703, and 79.703 MHz. The 79.703 MHz median gives 234.24 iterations per second and falls short of the approximately 84.38 MHz acceptance threshold for this cycle count. Option 0 used 5,774 logic cells, 1,245 registers, and six block RAMs. The median placement's critical path crosses EX branch resolution into fetch replay invalidation.\n- Local formal is incomplete. The initial liveness failure is described above. The recovery fix passed liveness on subsequent variants. The full run on the selected RTL remained in progress at handoff.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
