{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'r6s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; j=json.load(sys.stdin); print(j[\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(j[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': 'The previous predictor queue saved 7.91% of stalled CoreMark cycles,\\nand its 66.933 MHz placement median limited the score to 168.49 iter/s.\\nReplay of 592,690 marked CoreMark control transfers yielded 85,241\\nsuccessor misses for the incumbent table and 43,621 for a 128-entry\\nhashed target table with two-bit direction hysteresis. A registered\\ndecode head can absorb ready fetches during backend holds and feed\\nEX a stable successor without a queue lookup.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Index a 128-entry direct-mapped target table with PC[8:2] XOR PC[15:9].\\nStore PC[31:9] as the tag, a conditional bit and the taken target's\\n14-bit word offset within the source's 64 KiB page. Rebuild the\\ntarget from the current source page. The hash and tag together\\nidentify the full source PC. Predict a conditional target only when\\nthe two-bit counter at the same index votes taken. Predict a jump\\ntarget on a valid hit. Use full-width PC+4 otherwise. EX compares\\nthe full predicted successor for every advancing instruction,\\nincluding non-control instructions that encounter a stale entry.\\n\\nRegister the training valid, source index, tag, source page\\nand branch class and outcome as EX advances. Reuse the independently\\ncaptured resolved PC as the write data on the next edge. Write the\\ntarget entry on a legal taken branch or jump within its source page,\\nand clear that entry's valid bit for a taken cross-page transfer.\\nTrain conditional counters on both outcomes, saturating from\\n0 to 3 and starting from 1. Counters can share state across hash\\ncollisions. Reset the valid maps and keep the predictor arrays\\neligible for asynchronous-read distributed RAM.\\n\\nRun fetch ahead through a registered instruction head and a 16-slot\\ndistributed-RAM tail with one ring slot reserved for full detection.\\nStore the 32-bit instruction and its 30-bit predicted successor per\\nresponse. Normal decode reads the registered head. Advance the request\\nPC on a ready response with available space. At full occupancy the\\nnext dequeue opens\n…[4646 of 7718 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b126dffcf714edaa]…\net, cross-page invalidation, full-width fall-through and alignment traps, a full ring during a data hold, stale predictions, and a 10-cycle redirect-to-division retirement gap.\n- The ISS comparison passed for selftest and all three random programs in both zero-wait and dual-bus stall modes. CoreMark passed CRC validation with both buses stalled and completed 10 iterations in 3,994,772 marked cycles.\n- `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` reported 70.013, 73.253 and 73.253 MHz for Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2. The median gives 183.37 iterations/sec, 6.88% above 171.57. Option 0 used 5,922 LUT4s and 144 LUT RAM cells. Its longest setup path runs from the recovery flag to the head payload. The divider limits options 1 and 2.\n- The local single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` run passed all 53 checks on the final RTL, including liveness and both PC-continuity checks. The register consistency check took 16 minutes and 50 seconds in the stock Bitwuzla run and also passed independently with ABC `bmc3` over 21 frames.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
