lt selection and next-PC comparisons raised median Gowin Fmax from 61.366 to 66.022 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\nhyp-20261120-002-r5s1 | Split divider feedback by divisor magnitude | 167.44 | 72.226 | 4313521 | regression | - 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r5s1 (regression, +9.19%): Bounding the remainder to 12 bits for divisor magnitudes below 4096 and skipping 12 leading zero quotient bits at or above 4096 removes the cascaded full-width radix-8 steps within the eight-edge transfer schedule, shifting Gow\n…[1932 of 5004 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:cdef6347c37e6de2]…\nst_q` capped median Gowin Fmax at 80.93 MHz, below the 84.45 MHz needed for acceptance.\nhyp-20261120-002-r10s1 | Bypass load results into dependent address and branch lanes | 229.59 | 76.762 | 3343491 | regression | - 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r10s1 (regression, -3.15%): A parallel-carry AGU for loads dependent on `LW` at offsets 0–3 kept the data BRAM address registered, and the combined address and BEQ/BNE bypasses cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.55%, with the implied median Gowin Fmax of 76.76 MHz falling below the 79.26 MHz needed to match the champion.\nhyp-20261120-002-r11s1 | Move replay collisions off the fetch-to-decode datapath | 248.09 | 87.827 | 3540112 | regression | - 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r11s1 (regression, +4.65%): Keeping fill metadata paired with the replay BRAM output through FIFO stalls lets a same-row reread replace last-fill forwarding, and with registered recovery this preserved CoreMark's 3,540,112 marked cycles while EX result selection capped median Gowin Fmax at 87.827 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
