om Gowin's worst-path reports as median Fmax rose from 45.180 to 49.878 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (improvement, +17.24%): Registering the next-PC mismatch and capturing the resolved target independently cuts the EX-to-ID redirect path, while annulling the younger EX slot as the target enters ID/EX keeps misses to one bubble, letting the tagg\n…[10679 of 13751 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:25efeed7cd209b8c]…\n120-001-r13s0 (regression, -2.18%): Moving the result OR past EX/MEM adds parallel terms to the one-hot operand forwarding muxes, and the full routed path through forwarding and the ALU carry chain grew from 16.201 to 16.444 ns on Gowin, cutting CoreMark throughput at unchanged cycle count.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r13s1 (regression, +0.41%): Forwarding the registered low-half MUL product from MEM/WB into MEM let adjacent dependent ADDs and stores dependent only on write data issue without bubbles, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles by 2.69%, with fetch replay and its issue feedback capping median Gowin Fmax at 89.587 MHz against the 93.329 MHz needed for acceptance.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r13s2 (regression, -2.07%): Bypassing the load-use interlock for BEQ/BNE with registered rollback cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 3.78%, with replay-to-FIFO issue feedback and nearly tied EX forwarding paths into the ALU and divider capping median Gowin Fmax at 86.390 MHz against the 88.22 MHz needed to break even.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
