gnitude\nfits in 4 and 8 bits. Derive these flags directly from the raw divisor\nand operation signedness, using the all-ones upper slice and nonzero\nlow slice for negative inputs. The next work edge uses the registered\nflags for prefix selection. Preserve the existing arithmetic, eight-\nedge divider transfer, ALTOPS formulas, and held-result protocol.\n\nA scratch build placed at 76.583, 78.588, and 78.588 MHz with 4,925\nLUT4s. The remaining worst setup path is from a \n…[5558 of 8630 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ddc89147a0dec2ae]…\nThe three local Gowin placements measured 85.339, 89.691, and 89.691 MHz with 5,659 LUT4s and 971 registers, 32 more than the champion. The 89.691 MHz median gives 257.06 CoreMark iterations per second and falls short of the locally reproduced 91.689 MHz champion result and the 95.91 MHz promotion threshold. The median placement's forwarding path from ID/EX through the ALU reaches the integer result register at 16.444 ns. The champion's worst path reached the EX/MEM result register at 16.201 ns.\n\nThe direct split had the highest median of the tested split variants. Folding the integer mask into opcode selection measured 88.013 MHz, and explicitly grouping the ALU's one-hot operation terms measured 88.380 MHz. A parallel three-input MEM writeback mux measured 88.720 MHz. Folding reset into the EX result masks measured 79.745 MHz.\n\nThe stock single-channel ALTOPS run is incomplete. Fifty-two of 53 checks passed. `reg_ch0` was still solving step 20 after more than 26 minutes, with no verdict or counterexample.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
