{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r4s1.yaml && git status --short --untracked-files=all\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-002-r4s1\ntitle: \"Separate execute paths for memory and branch prediction\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 138.16 CoreMark iter/sec at 59.595 MHz over\n  4,313,521 marked cycles. Its reported worst setup path reaches data\n  BRAM through ID, EX and the integer ALU. A load or store always adds\n  forwarded rs1 to the decoded immediate, yet its address passes through\n  the general ALU and the EX/MEM result mux. A scratch build with a\n  separate address lane bypassed that mux and exposed two nearly tied\n  limits at 58.365 MHz median. One ran from WB forwarding into the\n  predictor table write, and the other ran through two cascaded radix-8\n  divider digits. The execute boundary needs to cut both exposed paths\n  to turn the shorter BRAM address path into a clock gain.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a full 32-bit effective address field to EX/MEM, fed directly by\n  forwarded rs1 plus the sign-extended I/S immediate. Reuse the existing\n  rs1-plus-immediate adder that computes the JALR target. Tap the raw sum\n  before JALR clears bit zero and before target selection. Clock the\n  address with the existing EX/MEM hold on data-memory backpressure.\n  Drive the dmem address directly from this field. The address is live\n  only for valid load/store requests, so its data input can sample the\n  raw sum without selecting on opcode, branch recovery or M-unit\n  completion. Use the same registered address for alignment, lane masks,\n  subword load extraction and the RVFI memory address.\n\n  Capture the predictor training valid bit and source PC on the EX\n  advance that resolves the transfer. Update the tagged last-successor\n  table on the following edge, using the target already sampled in\n  recovery_pc_q on the EX advance. The pending update consumes the\n  prior sampled target even when EX supplies another target at the\n  write edge. Clear pending training on reset. Preserve the predictor's\n  lookup policy and keep immediate registered recovery with its existing\n  single-bubble redirect and imem backpressur\n…[2359 of 5431 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2a32008b8f4939ae]…\nile: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Register the predictor training enable and source PC. Use the previously captured recovery target for the deferred table write. Preserve fetch lookup, recovery priority and the current redirect timing.\"\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: \"Use an eight-bit prefix step followed by six parallel radix-16 digits. Prepare odd multiples on the prefix edge and correct the held result sign on transfer. Keep the eight-edge handshake, RV32M edge cases and ALTOPS semantics.\"\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: \"Retain existing arithmetic and exact-latency assertions. Add prefix-boundary cases around divisors 15, 16, 255 and 256 for both quotient and remainder.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Cover an immediately forwarded base with a negative subword offset through dmem stalls. Check stable bus payload and contiguous retirement. Exercise consecutive resolvable transfers to check that deferred training writes pair each PC with its own target.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
