{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261120-001-r5s0\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; d=json.loads(sys.stdin.readline()); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(d.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\")); print({k:d[k] for k in (\\\"seeds\\\",\\\"perturbations\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"fitness\\\")})'\"", "aggregated_output": "Shorten the arithmetic paths into and out of EX/MEM without adding a\nstage or a bubble. Select the extended load value or the EX register\nresult before the MEM/WB edge and latch one final write value. Drive\nwriteback and MEM/WB forwarding directly from that register. When\ndmem stalls, hold the registered value, destination tag, and forwarding\neligibility while clearing retirement valid. A held consumer can then\nstill forward from a producer that retired during the stall.\n\nTap the raw forwarded rs1 plus immediate sum already computed for JALR\nand register it as the full 32-bit effective address under the existing\nEX/MEM hold. Preserve bit zero for the byte address and clear it only\nfor the JALR target. Use the effective address for the bus, alignment,\nload extraction, store masks, and RVFI. Select the ALU, link, multiply,\nand divide register results with parallel one-hot masks driven by\ndecoded operation type. Apply EX validity, stalls, and traps to the\nside-effect controls and M-unit handshakes. Preserve the predictor,\nforwarding priority, memory handshakes, and single-channel retirement.\n\nShorten the divider feedback with one exact parallel radix-16 digit\nper work edge, following the radix change exercised in r4s1. After\ncapturing operand magnitudes,\nconsume the top byte on the first work edge. Use a four-bit quotient\nand remainder lookup for the first nibble and a parallel narrow trial\nsubtraction for the second nibble. Only divisors through 15 and 255\nrespe