{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_27", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261120-00[23]-r[45]s[12]\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; exec(\\\"for s in sys.stdin:\\\\n d=json.loads(s); print(d[\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\"],d[\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\"],d[\\\\\\\"fmax_mhz\\\\\\\"],d[\\\\\\\"cycles\\\\\\\"]); print(d.get(\\\\\\\"implementation_notes\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"))\\\")'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 Separate execute paths for memory and branch prediction 66.022 4313521\n# Bench execute path separation\n\n## RTL and tests\n\n- EX/MEM registers the raw forwarded `rs1 + imm` sum under the existing data-memory hold. JALR masks bit zero only on its control-target path. MEM uses the registered address for the bus, alignment, byte lanes, load extraction and word-aligned RVFI reporting.\n- IF samples the training source PC and pending bit alongside the existing recovery target. The next edge writes the tagged entry from the prior sampled pair. Reset clears pending training and entry validity. Fetch lookup and registered redirect timing retain the existing policy.\n- The blocking divider uses a nibble lookup and a byte-wide trial on its prefix edge, then one parallel radix-16 digit on each of six edges. Odd multiples are prepared on the prefix edge from the captured divisor. Sign correction uses the held quotient or remainder, and the result still transfers eight edges after capture. ALTOPS retains its existing formulas and transfer timing.\n- The directed tests cover every first nibble for divisors 0 through 16, quotient and remainder around the byte-prefix limit, a stalled byte store and dependent load using forwarded bases with negative offsets, and back-to-back training of distinct JAL and JALR successors.\n\n## Timing adjustments and results\n\nThe initial implementation reached 61.366 MHz median and was limited by the integer result path. EX now selects integer, link, multiply and divide results in parallel on the same writeback lane. EX also compares the relative and JALR candidates with the fetch PC, then selects the comparison result using the decoded target type. These changes shorten the result and redirect logic while retaining the existing forwarding priority and branch behavior.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 63.528, 66.022 and 66.022 MHz. Option 0 used 4,837 logic cells, 980 registers, 64 LUT-RAMs, six BSRAMs and two DSPs. The worst paths at the median are redirect capture and in\n…[4896 of 7968 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ac87702c2f8affe0]…\nand without bus stalls. CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs with both stalls enabled in 4,313,521 marked cycles.\n- The preprocessed ALTOPS RTL matches the champion after stripping whitespace and comments. A Yosys SAT proof with a reset base case and temporal induction verified output equality under arbitrary requests and result backpressure.\n- Standalone SAT checks proved `trial = divisor × digit + remainder` and `remainder < divisor` for every legal trial in each of the four digit configurations.\n- Local `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL, including `reg_ch0`.\n- Gowin measured 67.669, 72.226, and 72.226 MHz, for a median of 72.226 MHz and 167.44 CoreMark iterations per second, 9.19% above the champion. The mapped design uses 4,295 LUT4s and 766 registers. The LUT4 count is 68 above the scratch result. The worst setup paths cross EX forwarding into the predictor.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r5s2 Run a hysteretic branch predictor ahead through a LUT RAM queue 66.933 3972442\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
