{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r6s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Divide with three divisor-width lanes\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion reaches 171.57 CoreMark iter/sec at 74.008 MHz.\n  Its longest reported setup path runs from divisor_q into the partial\n  remainder through the first-byte computation. That computation puts a\n  nibble lookup and a trial-subtract bank in series. Round 5's two-lane\n  experiment used two dependent digit steps for small divisors. An unsigned\n  divisor of at least 2^k guarantees that the first k quotient bits are\n  zero, while a divisor below 2^k bounds the remainder to k bits. Three\n  lanes use both bounds to extract one digit per work edge.\nhypothesis: |\n  Repartition the blocking divider into three restoring lanes selected by\n  unsigned divisor magnitude at capture. Reserve the first work edge for\n  quotient seeding and registration of divisor multiples. Each of the six\n  following work edges extracts a single quotient digit. Keep the divisor\n  multiples, partial remainder and dividend/quotient shift register local\n  to each lane. Latch the lane selection one-hot and run only that lane.\n\n  For magnitudes 1 through 255, seed from the top two dividend bits. A\n  divisor of one copies the two-bit prefix to the quotient and leaves a\n  zero remainder. A divisor of two splits the prefix into the high bit as\n  quotient and the low bit as remainder. A divisor of three produces digit\n  one only for prefix three. For any other nonzero divisor in the lane,\n  the prefix already fits as the remainder and the first two quotient bits\n  are zero. Process the remaining 30 bits in six radix-32 steps with an\n  8-bit remainder and a 13-bit trial.\n\n  For magnitudes 256 through 16,383, seed the remainder with the top eight\n  dividend bits and process six radix-16 digits with a 14-bit remainder\n  and an 18-bit trial. From 16,384 up, seed with the top 14 dividend bits\n  and process six radix-8 digits with a 32-bit remainder and a 35-bit\n  trial. The seeded quotient bits for both lanes are zero.\n\n  In each digit lane compare the trial against registered divisor\n  multiples in parallel. Use adjacent borrow flags to select the largest\n  fitting multiple's difference and quotient digit. Derive even multiples\n  by shifts. Form the odd multiples with constant shifts, adds and\n  subtracts on the seed edge. Use carry-save compression for positive\n  three-term sums. At the result boundary use parallel one-hot masks to\n  select the active lane's quotient or remainder, then apply the captured\n  sign. Handle a zero divisor with a held all-ones quotient or original\n  dividend.\n  Preserve signed overflow, the existing ALTOPS formulas, request/result\n  backpressure and reset, and the earliest transfer eight edges after\n  capture in both modes. Retire through the existing single RVFI channel.\n\n  The three-lane scratch build reached 75.509, 79.049 and 79.049 MHz in\n  Gowin, using 5,442 logic cells in placement option 0. The longest paths\n  now run through EX forwarding to branch recovery and predictor training.\n  At 4,313,521 marked cycles the measured median implies 183.26 iter/sec,\n  about 6.8% above the champion. The acceptance margin at that cycle\n  count requires a median above 77.41 MHz. Recheck the marked cycle count,\n  canonical CRCs and placement medians during implementation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n    description: >-\n      Replace the serial byte prefix and uniform radix-16 feedback with\n      divisor-width-selected seeds and three fixed-width single-digit\n      lanes. Share transaction control and sign correction. Preserve the\n      real and ALTOPS result timing, backpressure, and reset semantics.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: >-\n      Keep the existing arithmetic and handshake checks. Add quotient and\n      remainder vectors at and adjacent to the 2^8 and 2^14 lane boundaries,\n      using maximum dividends and dividends one away from exact multiples.\n      Exercise both signs and check result hold and reset in an active lane.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
