ing EX/MEM forwarding and retirement path when its result is available.\\nThe result stays valid until EX/MEM accepts it. Fast ALU operations and MUL*\\nretain their current latency. The launch, six computation cycles, and advance\\noccupy eight EX cycles for every divide or remainder, including special\\ncases. The seven added cycles leave room for a redirect or load-use bubble\\nin the existing ten-cycle formal liveness window. Use the existing ALTOPS\\nvalues with the same transaction latency and hold protocol so the formal\\ngate still exercises divider dependencies.\\n\\nThe profile suggests minimal impact on marked cycles. The divide before the\\nstart marker can shift the stall sequence, so remeasure marked cycles. At the\\nbaseline cycle count, 5.70 MHz clears t\n…[25183 of 28255 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d7792656bbfb84b9]…\nside the synchronous read and forward the captured write\\nwhen the two indices coincide. This gives deterministic replay across\\nthe block RAM's read-during-write cases. A pending redirect selects\\nthe corrected external fetch and disables replay until an accepted\\nword has realigned the replay read with the fetch PC. On reset and\\nthe existing non-training redirect invalidation, including FENCE.I,\\ngate replay immediately and scrub the packed valid bits through the\\nRAM write port while external fetch keeps running. Apply reset and\\ninvalidation before admitting subsequent fills.\\n\\nA scratch 512-entry version passed the canonical CoreMark CRCs in\\n3,540,112 marked cycles, down 5.81%. Gowin used 5,305 logic cells\\nand six block RAMs including the wrapper's original four. Its\\n77.493 MHz median yielded 218.90 iter/sec. This cycle count needs\\na median above 78.12 MHz to clear the 4.6% acceptance margin.\\nThe clock target is 79 MHz. The existing EX recovery and divider\\npaths limited different placements.\\n\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
