{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_50", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git log --all --oneline --grep='load-dependent\\\\|Resolve load\\\\|Register the resolved' | head -30\nrg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261120-001-r9s0\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; j=json.load(sys.stdin); print(j[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(j[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "Exempt aligned BEQ/BNE encodings from the adjacent load-use bubble.\nUse the existing EX/MEM producer tag match to recognize the dependency\nin EX. Qualify the producer's register-write eligibility before\nlatching EX/MEM by checking alignment with the low two bits of the\neffective address. This keeps the alignment check out of the ordinary\nEX forwarding mux and lets a branch behind a trapping load use the\nolder valid value. An unready load still holds EX through the existing\ndmem stall. Retain the load-use interlock for other consumers.\n\nFor the bypassed branch, register its dependency bits, polarity and\nother forwarded operand in EX/MEM. Compute its fall-through and\nrelative target in EX, compare both against the oldest unissued PC,\nand register the full-width match results with the candidates.\nIn MEM, compare the saved operand to the older load's width- and\nsign-extended value in MEM/WB. If both sources match the load tag,\nthe equality predicate is true. Use the resulting branch condition\nto select the precomputed match and resolved PC. A correct prediction\nlets younger instructions advance. A miss redirects through the\nexisting IF recovery input, kills the younger EX slot before any M\nrequest, store or writeback, and discards younger decode and queue\nentries. Give a MEM redirect priority over EX, and allow EX to use the\nsingle training port when an older late branch was correct. Insert\nthe actual compared operands and resolved PC into the branch's RVFI\nrecord when MEM/WB advances.\n\nA scratch MEM-compare run passed the canonical CRCs in 3,405,608\nmarked cycles. At that cycle count, the 4.6 percent acceptance margin\nrequires over 84.45 MHz median. Target 86 MHz for 252.5 CoreMark\niter/sec. Check that the registered load operand and early alignment\ncheck keep the new redirect arbitration within the clock target.\nVerify all three Gowin placements, canonical stalled CoreMark, and\nthe unmodified correctness gates. Exercise both compare polarities,\nsigned and unsigned subword loads, held dmem, both sources matching,\ntr\n…[1110 of 4182 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6a6bf566d9b0821c]…\nnterexample when a late miss targeted a divide. The branch now completes retirement while IF presents the registered recovery address. The corrected path issues on the same edge as before, and the divider keeps its existing schedule. Directed tests cover this sequence, predictor arbitration, held subword loads, both branch polarities, both sources matching, trapping loads, and wrong-path stores and RV32M requests.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- Verilator lint passed.\n- All eight cocotb suites and all five co-simulation ELFs passed. Canonical stalled CoreMark passed in 3,402,673 marked cycles for ten iterations, 3.88% fewer cycles than the champion.\n- The final formal run has passed 52 of 53 checks, including BEQ, BNE, liveness, and both PC consistency checks. The register proof is still running. No counterexample has been reported for this revision.\n- The measured cycle count requires about 84.38 MHz median Fmax to clear the acceptance margin. Gowin option 0 routed at 80.009 MHz. Options 1 and 2 are still running.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
