{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=[json.loads(i) for i in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor r in rows:\n if r['category']=='predictor' or r['id'].endswith(('r8s1','r9s0','r10s0')):\n  print('\\\\n',r['id'],r['title'],r['fitness'],r.get('cycles'),r.get('critical_path'))\n  if r['id'].endswith(('r1s2','r5s2','r6s2','r8s1','r9s2','r10s2')):\n   print(r.get('motivation'))\n   print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n   print(str(r.get('implementation_notes'))[:6000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 Predict fetch with a tagged last-next-PC table 13.14 4220145 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_2_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -192.449, 'arrival_ns': 202.744, 'logic_cells': 158, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nBench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\n\nAdd a 64-entry direct-mapped last-next-PC table in IF. Index it with PC[7:2] and store {PC[31:8], successor[31:2]} in one 54-bit payload per entry. Both low successor bits are zero in this RV32IM core. Use a resettable valid vector and an unreset payload array with one asynchronous read and one synchronous write for RAM inference. A valid tag hit predicts the stored successor, and a miss predicts PC+4. Train at the EX PC only when a valid, non-trapping conditional branch, JAL, or JALR advances out of EX. Store the resolved successor on every such advance, including the fall-through of a not-taken conditional branch.\n\nOn acceptance into ID/EX, the fetch PC advances to the predicted successor. While an older memory operation stalls EX, hold both ID/EX and the fetch PC and defer training and correction. The current fetch PC therefore supplies the prediction associated with the instruction in EX. Compare it with the architectural successor on every valid EX advance, including for non-control instructions so a stale entry can never let a wrong-path instruction issue. Correct mismatches ahead of all fetch holds and squash the younger fetch-to-ID slot. Preserve the existing trap fall-through, link value, and resolved successor reported by RVFI. On a correct prediction, accep\n…[26842 of 29914 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:793662a9287e88ce]…\ns, plus the stalled CoreMark CRC check. All eight cocotb suites passed on this RTL before the final two predictor cases were added. The expanded suite passed on a later timing trial, and its rerun on the selected RTL was still in progress at handoff.\n- Stalled CoreMark completed ten iterations in 3,402,673 marked cycles. The canonical CRCs and validation message matched, and the simulator reported no out-of-bounds accesses.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 82.478, 79.703, and 79.703 MHz. The 79.703 MHz median gives 234.24 iterations per second and falls short of the approximately 84.38 MHz acceptance threshold for this cycle count. Option 0 used 5,774 logic cells, 1,245 registers, and six block RAMs. The median placement's critical path crosses EX branch resolution into fetch replay invalidation.\n- Local formal is incomplete. The initial liveness failure is described above. The recovery fix passed liveness on subsequent variants. The full run on the selected RTL remained in progress at handoff.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
