{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r[\\\"id\\\"].endswith((\\\"r11s2\\\",\\\"r11s1\\\",\\\"r11s0\\\",\\\"r9s2\\\",\\\"r8s1\\\",\\\"r10s2\\\")):\n  print(r.keys() if r[\\\"id\\\"].endswith('r11s2') else '', r['id'])\n  for k,v in r.items():\n   if k not in {'id','title','category','motivation'}:\n    print(k, repr(v)[:12000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": " hyp-20261120-002-r8s1\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 210.96 CoreMark iter/sec at 79.292 MHz over\\n3,758,572 marked cycles. Its learned stream table only opens replay\\nafter a BTB hit, and a replayed word must enter the FIFO before decode\\ncan use it. Profiling found about 272,000 cycles where the issue queue\\nwas empty and no external instruction word was ready. A separate\\nsynchronous replay lookup one cycle ahead of the fetch queue can cover\\nmore of those cycles and take the bulk of the replay storage out of LUT RAM.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Replace the stream-relative replay stage with a synchronous block-RAM\\nlookup keyed by the fetch PC. Start with 512 entries containing an\\ninstruction word, the full remaining PC tag, and a valid bit. Whenever\\nfetch accepts the current word, use the same predicted successor that\\nupdates the fetch PC to read the next replay entry on that edge. Hold\\nthe RAM output and its collision metadata when fetch holds. The ready\\nexternal response and registered replay result become two producers of\\nthe existing fall-through FIFO. A ready response has priority. When\\nthe external bus stalls, a valid, exact-tag replay hit can bypass an\\nempty FIFO directly into ID or enqueue if decode is blocked. Preserve\\nFIFO order and the existing BTB prediction and recovery policy.\\n\\nRegister the FIFO head PC separately from its instruction payload so\\nEX successor validation can read the oldest buffered address without\\na current-pointer RAM access. On a pop, promote the next queued PC.\\nOn a push into an empty queue or a same-edge replacement of the final\\nentry, capture the incoming PC. When the queue is empty or discarded,\\nEX sees the current fetch PC, even when the instruction is unavailable.\\n\\nFill only from accepted ready-bus words. Capture the write index and\\nentry alongside the synchronous read and forward the captured write\\nwhen the two indices coincide. This gives deterministic replay across\\nthe block RAM's read-during-write cases. A pending redirect selects\\nth\n…[48407 of 51479 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:79f9e4b94e51e915]…\nh NRET=1` reported 249.64 iterations/sec, 5.31% above the champion and above the 247.96 acceptance threshold. Gowin options 0, 1, and 2 reached 82.049, 87.101, and 87.101 MHz. Option 0 used 5,823 logic cells and six block RAMs. The median critical path is EX forwarding into the ALU result register.\\n\\nDirection counters are shared by sources that hash to the same row. Corrected transfers with a nonsequential successor can need a second redirect. Both cases are covered by EX's full-width successor validation.\\n\"\ntimestamp '2026-11-20T14:02:53.620211Z'\nslot 2\nround_id 11\nlesson '- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r11s2 (improvement, +5.31%): Registering the XOR index alongside the fetch PC and using PC+4 on the first corrected fetch kept the index hash and recovery mux out of the predictor lookup feeding replay BRAM, raising median Gowin Fmax from 83.921 to 87.101 MHz, while the 128-entry taken-target table with two-bit direction counters retained loop targets across exits and cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 1.44%.'\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
