{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor i in (32,34):\n d=json.loads(Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()[i-1]);print(d['id']);print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis']);print(d['implementation_notes'][:3100])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r11s0\nRegister the potential target miss, the fall-through miss, and the\nactual aligned target direction independently at the EX/IF edge.\nQualify both miss bits with EX advance and force both on FENCE.I.\nCheck all retiring instructions against the oldest unissued PC as\nbefore, including ordinary instructions at a stale predictor entry.\nRetain full-address comparison and the existing JALR bit-zero and\nmisalignment behavior. Capture the selected correction word PC in\nparallel. IF selects the registered miss using the registered\ndirection and uses the captured address on that same recovery cycle.\nKeep the correction pending through imem backpressure. Let a ready\ncorrected instruction bypass an empty queue and squash the younger\nEX slot until decode issues the corrected instruction. This removes\nthe combinational EX redirect gate on ID/EX acceptance. Start replay\nscrub from the registered non-training redirect, mask stale replay\non that recovery cycle, and hold fills during scrub.\n\nStore replay-index equality and full word-PC equality beside each\nstaged tagged successor entry. On an accepted lookahead read, latch\nthe collision and exact forwarding hit from the predictor hit, those\nrelation bits, and the actual fill enable. The sequential fallback\nalways changes replay index. Hold collision metadata and the last\nfill word with the RAM response when fetch holds. An exact collision\nuses the accepted bus word. A same-index different-tag collision\nsuppresses the old RAM row. A noncollision uses the normal full tag\nand valid check. Preserve ready-bus priority and FIFO order.\n\nRegister the producer-qualified one-hot operand bypass selects in ID\nso the EX predicate and arithmetic start from the operand mux.\nCompare against the producers about to enter EX/MEM and MEM/WB.\nQualify with their actual next write enables after recovery, legality\nand alignment checks, give the younger producer priority and retain\nthe masks with a held consumer. Keep the captured M operands and the\nMEM/WB forwarding payload through t\n…[8869 of 11941 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ae333fb6a66402bf]…\nlving at step 20 near the implementation deadline. Unchanged proof input also reached the six-minute diagnostic limit with Boolector and Yices. No run produced a counterexample, and no formal fixes were needed for the completed checks.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` passed for selftest and all three random programs with both zero-wait and stalled buses. CoreMark completed ten iterations in 3,489,128 marked cycles, a 1.44% reduction, with the required CRCs and validation banner.\n- `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1` reported 249.64 iterations/sec, 5.31% above the champion and above the 247.96 acceptance threshold. Gowin options 0, 1, and 2 reached 82.049, 87.101, and 87.101 MHz. Option 0 used 5,823 logic cells and six block RAMs. The median critical path is EX forwarding into the ALU result register.\n\nDirection counters are shared by sources that hash to the same row. Corrected transfers with a nonsequential successor can need a second redirect. Both cases are covered by EX's full-width successor validation.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
