{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261120-00[12]-r(9|10|13)s[01]' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; rows=map(json.loads,sys.stdin); [print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],r[\\\"cycles\\\"],r[\\\"motivation\\\"] if \\\"motivation\\\" in r else r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"motivation\\\"]) for r in rows]'\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261120-001-r9s0 Resolve load-dependent equality branches from registered load data 3402673 The champion retired 135,320 BEQ/BNE instructions that immediately\nconsumed a load in the 3,540,112-cycle CoreMark interval. A scratch\ncombinational bypass passed the canonical CRCs in 3,406,310 cycles,\nbut reached only 58.434 MHz in Gowin placement option 1 because the\ndata memory path extended through EX into fetch recovery. Resolving\nthis branch in MEM can use the already registered load result while\neliminating the issue bubble.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r9s1 Resolve load-dependent equality branches in a late branch lane 3405608 A profile of the 3,540,112-cycle champion found 203,924 load-use\nbubbles, including 135,260 before BEQ or BNE. An equality branch can\nissue while its older load is in MEM and compare against the registered\nload result one cycle later. A scratch combinational MEM-to-EX bypass\nsaved cycles but clocked at only 64.369 MHz in Gowin option 1. Put the\nload response and the branch comparison on opposite sides of a register.\n\nhyp-20261120-001-r10s0 Resolve adjacent load branches with width-aware registered comparisons 3402673 The round 9 late BEQ/BNE lane cut marked CoreMark cycles from\n3,540,112 to 3,402,673. Its 84.28 MHz median left the score just below\nthe acceptance threshold. A fresh stalled trace of the champion\nreproduced its cycle count and CRCs. All 135,320 adjacent load-dependent\nBEQ/BNE retirements in the interval used a byte or halfword load, or\ncompared a word load to x0. A width-aware lane can cover those cases\nwith at most 16 bits of late comparison. Adjacent load-to-store data\ndependencies occurred only 420 times in the same trace.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r10s1 Bypass load results into dependent address and branch lanes 3343491 The champion delivers 237.06 CoreMark iter/sec at 83.921 MHz over\n3,540,112 marked cycles. Of the measured load-use interlocks, 135,260\npreceded BEQ or BNE and 59,266 reflected a true load-to-load address\ndependency. Every producer in those load pairs was LW. Their dependent\nloads used immediate offsets of 0, 1, or 2. Round 9 saved 3.88% of\ncycles by letting equality branches issue behind loads, and fell short\nof acceptance at 84.28 MHz. Covering dependent addresses with the same\nlate operand path gives the design more timing headroom.\n\nhyp-20261120-001-r13s0 Move integer result selection after the EX/MEM register 3489128 Gowin's median placements for the 91.689 MHz champion show a path from\nID/EX through forwarding and the integer ALU carry chain into the EX/MEM\nresult. The sum still crosses six LUTs after the carry chain. Clocking\nthe integer result separately gives the late ALU data a shorter route to\nthe register and leaves the result available for next-cycle forwarding.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r13s1 Complete adjacent MUL consumers in the memory stage 3395412 The champion scores 262.78 CoreMark iterations/sec at 91.689 MHz in\n3,489,128 marked cycles. Round 12 moved low MUL completion to MEM and\nsaved 58,213 cycles by letting independent successors issue. A fresh\nmarked RVFI trace found 93,960 low MULs: 58,320 followed by independent\nADDs, 32,400 by ADDs consuming the product and 3,240 by stores consuming\nit as write data. None of those dependent ADDs had an immediate consumer.\nThe operands that still cause bubbles are first needed at a stage that\ncan use the registered product directly.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
