{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r14s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-003-r14s2\ntitle: Predict load values for adjacent branches and loads\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion spends 3,489,128 marked CoreMark cycles at 91.689 MHz.\n  Prior profiles counted about 135,000 adjacent load-dependent BEQ/BNEs\n  and 59,000 load-dependent loads. Round 13 used branch prediction to\n  recover most of the first group, but its late branch validator brought\n  a second recovery and training lane into the timed core. Predicting\n  the older load result can let both classes use ordinary execution\n  and the same registered validation.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a small tagged, confidence-gated last-value predictor trained by\n  completed loads. Read the prediction in parallel with EX and register\n  the candidate with the producer in EX/MEM. For a confident nonzero\n  destination, let an immediately dependent legal BEQ/BNE or load enter\n  ID/EX without the load-use bubble. Forward the candidate only to that\n  adjacent consumer. Keep the actual load request and standard formatted\n  MEM/WB writeback, and validate the candidate against the load response\n  when the bus accepts it. Capture the mismatch at the MEM edge so that\n  the response never enters EX's operand or successor paths.\n\n  A hit lets the consumer progress through existing EX and use its\n  ordinary recovery and RVFI path. A registered mismatch or a producer\n  trap discards the speculative consumer before it can access data\n  memory or retire, squashes its younger ID/EX instruction and ignores\n  any successor validation or training from that consumer. Replay at\n  the consumer's saved PC using the producer's real writeback, or its\n  preceding architectural value if the producer trapped. Keep predictor\n  rollback separate from instruction replay invalidation. The ordinary\n  load-use interlock still applies on a miss or low confidence and to\n  other dependent opcodes. A stalled producer holds both the prediction\n  and its consumer. Train only on legal, nonsquashed accepted loads.\n\n  Target about 170,000 net saved marked cycles at the champion's clock,\n  which would yield about 276 iterations/sec. Measure prediction\n  coverage, accuracy, rollback cost, marked cycles, and the three-option\n  Gowin median. Check adjacent chains, producer faults, bus stalls,\n  competing branch redirects and wrong-path memory suppression with\n  the existing single-channel correctness gates.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Exempt only eligible dependent consumers with a live confident load prediction. Preserve all data bus and unrelated issue interlocks.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the prediction and the speculative consumer marker across the corresponding pipeline edges.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Capture the producer prediction and forward it to the marked adjacent consumer. Qualify effects with registered rollback.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Compare the predicted and actual load values at completion and register the verdict. Suppress speculative consumer bus effects and retirement on failure.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect predictor lookup, resolved-load training, issue eligibility, and rollback controls.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Prioritize rollback to the consumer PC over younger recovery and suppress invalid predictor training while retaining instruction replay.\n  - file: rtl/load_value_predictor.sv\n    description: Implement the tagged load value table and confidence update from accepted loads.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Cover validated speculation, value mismatches, dependency chains, producer traps, and stalls during rollback.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
