{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; keys=(\\\"id\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\"); [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(f\\\"{k}: {str(d.get(k))[:6200]}\\\" for k in keys),\\\"\\\\n\\\",list(d)) for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\ntitle: Predict fetch with a tagged last-next-PC table\nmotivation: Bench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\n\nhypothesis: {'motivation': 'Bench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Add a 64-entry direct-mapped last-next-PC table in IF. Index it with PC[7:2] and store {PC[31:8], successor[31:2]} in one 54-bit payload per entry. Both low successor bits are zero in this RV32IM core. Use a resettable valid vector and an unreset payload array with one asynchronous read and one synchronous write for RAM inference. A valid tag hit predicts the stored successor, and a miss predicts PC+4. Train at the EX PC only when a valid, non-trapping conditional branch, JAL, or JALR advances out of EX. Store the resolved successor on every such advance, including the fall-through of a not-taken conditional branch.\\n\\nOn acceptance into ID/EX, the fetch PC advances to the predicted successor. While an older memory operation stalls EX, hold both ID/EX and the fetch PC and defer training and correction. The current fetch PC therefore supplies the prediction associated with the instruction in EX. Compare it with the architectural successor on e\n…[8790 of 11862 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9fd53b3589bdc29b]…\nALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks on the first run.\n- All six cocotb runners passed.\n- ISS co-simulation passed selftest and all three random traces with and without both bus stalls. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC check with 4,220,145 marker-bracketed cycles under both stalls.\n- `make fpga TARGET=bench` scored 117.93 iterations/sec, above the 105.22 threshold. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 45.530, 49.767, and 49.767 MHz. Option 0 used 3,714 LUT4s and its longest reported path crossed the ALU to dmem.\n\nNo correctness issues surfaced in the local checks. The tests ran in a temporary staging directory.\n \n ['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson']\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
