{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'date -u && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r3s2.yaml && tail -7 /srv/hwebench/tmp/4876824c/8df95641/bench_profile/formal_mapped_p14.log'", "aggregated_output": "Fri Nov 20 03:29:22 UTC 2026\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r3s2\ntitle: Run the next-PC predictor ahead of decode\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's tagged successor predictor reduced redirect stalls, but\n  it still advances only when ID accepts an instruction. A ready imem\n  cycle during a load-use bubble or data-bus hold goes unused. Running\n  the predictor ahead would let fetch follow trained branches during\n  those holds and deliver queued instructions during later imem stalls.\nhypothesis: |\n  Run a single speculative fetch stream using the existing tagged\n  successor predictor and a four-entry fall-through queue of instruction,\n  fetch PC, and predicted successor. Perform the table lookup from the\n  request PC in parallel with imem. Advance the request PC to that\n  prediction only when imem is ready and the instruction is either\n  consumed directly by ID or enqueued. If the queue is full, permit a new\n  request to be accepted on a simultaneous dequeue. Derive the ID input\n  and ID-side hazards from the selected queue head, or the live response\n  when empty. With an empty queue and both ends ready, retain one-cycle\n  fetch-to-ID transfer.\n\n  Carry each accepted instruction's own predicted successor into EX and\n  compare it with the architectural successor on every valid EX advance.\n  Train the existing table only on advancing, non-trapping control\n  transfers. Registered recovery discards all younger queue entries and\n  annuls the younger ID/EX slot, clearing its valid bit even if a\n  data-bus hold prevents the rest of ID/EX from advancing. Fetch from\n  the registered correction address immediately. When imem accepts the\n  correction, let ID consume the corrected response directly or enqueue\n  it if ID is held. Keep the correction address selected across imem\n  backpressure. A ready corrected instruction can enter ID on the\n  recovery cycle.\n  Preserve full-width, word-aligned addresses, architectural trap\n  handling, and actual RVFI successor reporting.\n\n  A temporary four-entry prototype completed mar\n…[1138 of 4210 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:051f8854f0866318]…\nstered wrong-path squash.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire independent fetch and decode handshakes, queue head data, and EX recovery.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Use queue head availability for load-use detection and ID acceptance instead of raw imem readiness.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Verify queued delivery across bus stalls and discard multiple wrong-path entries on a trained branch or JALR mismatch. Exercise recovery when a stale prediction on a load leaves the data bus holding ID/EX. Check retirement and wrong-path memory effects independently of the previous exact fetch sequence.\nSBY  4:29:21 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:03:50 (230)\nSBY  4:29:21 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:03:35 (215)\nSBY  4:29:21 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\nSBY  4:29:21 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\nSBY  4:29:21 [reg_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\n\nFormal: 53 passed, 0 failed\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
