{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|hyp-20261120-00[123]-r[123]s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict fetch with a tagged last-next-PC table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Bench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Bench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 64-entry direct-mapped last-next-PC table in IF. Index it with PC[7:2] and store {PC[31:8], successor[31:2]} in one 54-bit payload per entry. Both low successor bits are zero in this RV32IM core. Use a resettable valid vector and an unreset payload array with one asynchronous read and one synchronous write for RAM inference. A valid tag hit predicts the stored successor, and a miss predicts PC+4. Train at the EX PC only when a valid, non-trapping conditional branch, JAL, or JALR advances out of EX. Store the resolved successor on every such advance, including the fall-through of a not-taken conditional branch.\\n\\nOn acceptance into ID/EX, the fetch PC advances to the predicted successor. While an older memory operation stalls EX, hold both ID/EX and the fetch PC and defer training and correction. The current fetch PC therefore supplies the prediction associated with the instruction in EX. Co\n…[22105 of 25177 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:77eb3f10d9377740]…\nrect queue head after a divider hold. Yosys had elaborated the unpacked array of packed structs as one 64-bit word. Declaring the entries explicitly as 64-bit vectors corrected the elaboration.\\n- With `NRET=1`, cosim passed selftest and all three random traces with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark produced the canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 4,008,388 marker-bracketed cycles.\\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 48.680, 49.663, and 49.663 MHz. Option 0 used 3,846 LUT4s. The median gives 123.90 iterations/sec, 0.55 iterations/sec above the 123.35 threshold.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T03:48:17.376300Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (regression, +5.06%): A two-entry fall-through queue let the tagged next-PC predictor fetch through load-use bubbles and data-bus holds, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.02%, with a median Gowin Fmax of 49.663 MHz against the 55.38 MHz needed at that cycle count to match the round-winning RV32M lane.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
