{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261120-001-r4s0\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(*(f\\\"{x}: {d[x]}\\\" for x in (\\\"fmax_mhz\\\",\\\"seeds\\\",\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\")),sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"fmax_mhz: 66.149\nseeds: [63.858, 66.149, 66.149]\ncycles: 4313521\ncritical_path: {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/partial_remainder_q_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_div/partial_remainder_q_9_s0', 'slack_ns': -10.66, 'arrival_ns': 20.954, 'logic_cells': 70, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_div']}\nimplementation_notes: # Bench operand delivery\n\nThe register file uses two 16-word Gowin distributed RAM banks with two asynchronous reads and one synchronous write. A shared resettable bitmap masks stale rows. Both ports retain same-cycle WB write-through, and x0 is hardwired.\n\nID compares both sources against the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags. The four nonzero matches use the same reset and hold as the ID/EX payload. EX qualifies them with the producer write controls and masks its three sources in youngest-first order. The held MEM/WB write control keeps forwarding available after retirement valid clears. Multiply and divide receive the forwarded operands at launch.\n\nThe ALU also folds the AUIPC PC and immediate choices into the operand masks. This removes the additional mux after forwarding. With that mux in place, the initial implementation reached a 59.994 MHz median and forwarding still appeared among the worst data BRAM paths. The final build is limited by paths internal to the divider. The tests cover both read ports in both banks, clearing previously written rows with reset, write-through, x0, youngest-writer priority, and both operands forwarding from an already-retired load across a data-memory hold.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. The RVFI channel-0 contract precheck and all seven cocotb runners passed.\n- Cosim passed for selftest and the three random programs with and without bus stalls, and for CoreMark with bus stalls. The local Makefile build uses `NRET=1` to match `core.yaml`.\n- The three Gowin placements reached 63.858, 66.149, and 66.149 MHz. The build reports 4,272 LUT4s, 784 registers, 88 LUT-RAM cells, and four BRAMs. The register file uses 32 of the RAM16SDP4 cells, covering both copies of each bank.\n- CoreMark validated all five canonical CRCs in 4,313,521 marked cycles. The 66.149 MHz median exceeds the 62.34 MHz acceptance margin and yields 153.35 iterations/s, up 11.00% from 138.16 iterations/s.\n- Single-retire ALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks on the final RTL with zero failures and no corrective retries.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
