{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_140", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,150p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r5s2.yaml && date -u\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r5s2\ntitle: \"Run a hysteretic branch predictor ahead through a LUT RAM queue\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The prior four-entry shift queue cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.91%,\n  but median Gowin frequency fell to 59.793 MHz. It shifted a 92-bit\n  packet per entry. In a retirement-order replay of 595,768 CoreMark\n  control transfers, a page-local tagged target table with two-bit\n  direction hysteresis cut misses from 85,882 to 58,171. Combining that\n  predictor with a LUT RAM queue can reduce redirect bubbles and absorb\n  bus stalls with narrower fetch packets and less shifting logic.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace last-successor prediction with a 64-entry direct-mapped target\n  table indexed by PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8]. Store PC[31:8], a conditional\n  bit and the target's 14-bit word offset within the source's 64 KiB page.\n  Validate the full source identity via the hashed index and tag. On a valid\n  unconditional hit or a conditional hit with counter MSB set, reconstruct\n  the target from the source page and stored offset. Otherwise use full-width\n  PC+4. Clear the indexed target-valid bit on cross-page training. Use a\n  separate two-bit saturating direction table at the same index. Treat its\n  first update after reset as starting from weakly not taken. Allow aliases\n  in the direction counters while still requiring a full target hit.\n\n  Register advancing EX training packets before committing table writes.\n  Train on legal, non-squashed control transfers with an aligned target.\n  For a conditional branch, record its taken target and update its counter\n  with the actual outcome, including a decrement on a fall-through. For\n  JAL and JALR, update the target entry as unconditional. Keep registered\n  recovery with its full-width resolved address and independent validity.\n\n  Run this predictor ahead of decode through a fall-through queue with a\n  dedicated registered head and a 16-entry circular tail in distributed\n  RAM. Store a 30-bit word PC and a 32-bit instruction per entry. Tail\n  pointers h\n…[3003 of 6075 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bc2b95ba97b912ca]…\nrify the latched prediction on every valid EX advance and report the architectural successor in RVFI.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Qualify decode acceptance and load-use detection with registered head availability. Keep the existing backend and data-memory stall rules.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire independent imem readiness and decode acceptance, latched prediction metadata, EX target and direction training, and registered recovery.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: >-\n      Cover full pop plus refill, decode during an imem stall, taken-taken-fall-through-taken\n      hysteresis, and full-queue flush under dmem backpressure with a cross-page redirect to DIV.\n      In the changed-target JALR test expect 0x14, 0x20, 0x24, 0x28, four requests at 0x48,\n      and 0x4C. In the stale-load test expect 0x04, 0x20, 0x08, 0x0C, 0x10, 0x14, and\n      0x18 for the second visit. Keep the existing retirement, timing, and side-effect assertions.\nFri Nov 20 04:49:11 UTC 2026\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
