{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|hyp-20261120-002-r6s1|hyp-20261120-003-r6s2|hyp-20261120-003-r5s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict fetch with a tagged last-next-PC table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Bench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Bench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 64-entry direct-mapped last-next-PC table in IF. Index it with PC[7:2] and store {PC[31:8], successor[31:2]} in one 54-bit payload per entry. Both low successor bits are zero in this RV32IM core. Use a resettable valid vector and an unreset payload array with one asynchronous read and one synchronous write for RAM inference. A valid tag hit predicts the stored successor, and a miss predicts PC+4. Train at the EX PC only when a valid, non-trapping conditional branch, JAL, or JALR advances out of EX. Store the resolved successor on every such advance, including the fall-through of a not-taken conditional branch.\\n\\nOn acceptance into ID/EX, the fetch PC advances to the predicted successor. While an older memory operation stalls EX, hold both ID/EX and the fetch PC and defer training and correction. The current fetch PC therefore supplies the prediction associated with the instruction in EX. Co\n…[63746 of 66818 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a811942863cf67e2]…\n73.253 MHz for Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2. The median gives 183.37 iterations/sec, 6.88% above 171.57. Option 0 used 5,922 LUT4s and 144 LUT RAM cells. Its longest setup path runs from the recovery flag to the head payload. The divider limits options 1 and 2.\\n- The local single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` run passed all 53 checks on the final RTL, including liveness and both PC-continuity checks. The register consistency check took 16 minutes and 50 seconds in the stock Bitwuzla run and also passed independently with ABC `bmc3` over 21 frames.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T06:35:20.642843Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r6s2 (regression, +6.88%): Bypassing the registered decode head into ID/EX on ready redirects avoids a refill cycle before division and preserves retirement liveness, while the divider capped median Gowin Fmax at 73.253 MHz, a large enough clock deficit against the four-entry fall-through queue to outweigh the lower stalled CoreMark cycle count.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
