{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (improvement, +729.95%): Replacing combinational `/` and `%` with a shared, blocking radix-4 divider removed the long EX ALU path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 45.180 MHz, with unchanged CoreMark cycles because no divide or remainder retired between the markers to incur the eight-cycle EX stall.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.42%): Avoiding redirects with a 64-entry tagged next-PC table cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.04%, with the combinational EX divider still limiting median Gowin Fmax to 5.546 MHz and leaving throughput well behind the round winner, whose clocked divider raised Fmax to 45.180 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.23%): Driving fetch directly from the registered EX/MEM redirect target while invalidating the younger EX slot preserved CoreMark's cycle count and removed the EX-to-IF-to-ID redirect path from Gowin's worst paths, leaving the register-file-to-data-memory path through the EX ALU to cap median Fmax at 47.542 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r2s1 (regression, +13.90%): A one-entry fall-through fetch buffer cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 3.07% by saving ready instructions during load-use and backend holds for later instruction-bus stalls, and using the registered full bit to select the decode payload while applying redirects to ID/EX validity removed the wide redirect path from Gowin's worst-path reports as median Fmax rose from 45.180 to 49.878 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (improvement, +17.24%): Registering the next-PC mismatch and capturing the resolved target independently cuts the EX-to-ID redirect path, while annulling the younger EX slot as the target enters ID/EX keeps misses to one bubble, letting the tagged successor predictor cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.04% as median Gowin Fmax rises from 45.180 to 49.767 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +9.88%): Registering the shared DSP product in EX/MEM exposed the divider’s three cascaded radix-4 steps as the criti\n…[3134 of 6206 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e24fb87c628a9450]…\n one-hot ALU enables, and divisor-width flags removed late decode from wide mux paths and raised median Gowin Fmax to 76.909 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles, with the divider byte prefix and forwarded branch target limiting different placements.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r6s1 (improvement, +12.40%): Checking the resolved successor against the FIFO head PC (fetch PC when empty) lets a four-entry fall-through LUT RAM queue absorb ready fetches during backend holds without storing predicted successors, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.91% and reaching 77.436 MHz median Gowin Fmax with the recovery mux after its candidate registers.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r6s2 (regression, +6.88%): Bypassing the registered decode head into ID/EX on ready redirects avoids a refill cycle before division and preserves retirement liveness, while the divider capped median Gowin Fmax at 73.253 MHz, a large enough clock deficit against the four-entry fall-through queue to outweigh the lower stalled CoreMark cycle count.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
