{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|hyp-20261120-002-r6s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [(lambda d: print(d[\\\"id\\\"], d[\\\"title\\\"], \\\"\\\\n\\\", {k:v for k,v in d.items() if k not in (\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"lesson\\\",\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"motivation\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"id\\\")},\\\"\\\\n\\\", d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])) (json.loads(l)) for l in sys.stdin]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 Predict fetch with a tagged last-next-PC table \n {'category': 'predictor', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 7, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'description': 'Implement the tagged successor table with EX training ports, select the successor at fetch acceptance, and give EX correction priority over the fetch hold.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Derive the true successor from the existing EX resolution and trap logic, compare it with the current fetch PC on valid EX advance, and emit the correction and table update. Use the true successor for RVFI.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': \"Wire the registered fetch PC into EX and route EX correction and training to IF. Keep the existing hazard unit's redirect handling driven by the correction pulse.\"}], 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 13.14, 'delta_pct': 8.42, 'vs_baseline': 8.42, 'fmax_mhz': 5.546, 'ipc_coremark': 2e-06, 'cycles': 4220145, 'iterations': 10, 'lut4': 12446, 'ff': 521, 'seeds': [5.065, 5.546, 5.546], 'perturbations': [[0, 5.065, 151], [1, 5.546, 155], [2, 5.546, 155]], 'lutram': 8, 'bsram': 8, 'dsp': 6, 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_2_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -192.449, 'arrival_ns': 202.744, 'logic_cells': 158, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}, 'logic_levels': 151, 'formal_passed': True, 'cosim_passed': True, 'error': None, 'timestamp': '2026-11-20T02:53:30.197100Z', 'slot': 2, 'round_id': 1} \n Add a 64-entry direct-mapped last-next-PC table in IF. Index it with PC[7:2] and store {PC[31:8], successor[31:2]} in one 54-bit payload per entry. Both low successor bits are zero in this RV32IM core. Use a resettable valid vector and an unreset payload array with one asynchronous read and one synchronous write for RAM inference. A valid tag hit predicts the stored successor, and a miss predicts PC+4. Train at the EX PC only when a valid, non-trapping conditional branch, JAL, or JALR advances out of EX. St\n…[32133 of 35205 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ea84e7c43925547b]…\nes against\nthe registered decode PC independently of this recovery selection.\nThe direct path preserves the retirement interval when a redirect\nis followed by division. Qualify writes, data-bus requests,\nM-unit launches and training with effective EX validity. Preserve\nfull-width architectural targets, alignment traps, actual RVFI\nsuccessors and single-channel retirement.\n\nA fully registered scratch version failed liveness when a redirect\nled to division. The recovery bypass passed the same liveness check\nand both PC-continuity checks. The tagged scratch prototype with\nthat bypass passed stalled CoreMark validation in 3,994,772 marked\ncycles, down 7.39%. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 66.587,\n74.278 and 74.278 MHz. The median yields 185.94 iter/s, up 8.37%\nfrom 171.57. At that cycle count the 4.6% acceptance margin requires\na median above 71.691 MHz. Option 0 used 5,880 LUT4s and 144 LUT RAM cells.\nSelftest and all three random programs matched the ISS with zero\nwait states and with both buses stalled.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
