{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|hyp-20261120-002-r8s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict fetch with a tagged last-next-PC table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Bench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Bench fetches sequentially until EX resolves a taken branch or jump, which squashes the fetch-to-ID slot. The marker-bracketed CoreMark retirement trace contained 357,527 nonsequential successors. Replaying that trace with a 64-entry tagged last-next-PC table reduced the estimated redirect count to about 85,000, capturing recurring loop targets, calls, and stable indirect jumps. The lookup can run from the registered fetch PC in parallel with the instruction-memory read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 64-entry direct-mapped last-next-PC table in IF. Index it with PC[7:2] and store {PC[31:8], successor[31:2]} in one 54-bit payload per entry. Both low successor bits are zero in this RV32IM core. Use a resettable valid vector and an unreset payload array with one asynchronous read and one synchronous write for RAM inference. A valid tag hit predicts the stored successor, and a miss predicts PC+4. Train at the EX PC only when a valid, non-trapping conditional branch, JAL, or JALR advances out of EX. Store the resolved successor on every such advance, including the fall-through of a not-taken conditional branch.\\n\\nOn acceptance into ID/EX, the fetch PC advances to the predicted successor. While an older memory operation stalls EX, hold both ID/EX and the fetch PC and defer training and correction. The current fetch PC therefore supplies the prediction associated with the instruction in EX. Co\n…[77947 of 81019 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:277a1ffcfb43b497]…\n53 checks, including liveness, forward and backward PC continuity, uniqueness, illegal instructions, causal ordering, and retirement cover. The selected RTL was also submitted to the unchanged suite. Neither run produced a counterexample, so no formal fix retries were needed.\\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 76.388, 78.390, and 78.390 MHz. Option 0 used 5,917 logic cells, 1,183 registers, and six block RAMs. The median and 3,528,765 marked cycles yield 222.15 iterations/s, 5.30% above the champion's 210.96 iterations/s. The median is 0.523 MHz above the required clock for a 4.6% improvement at this cycle count.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-20T09:24:48.034365Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r8s2 (regression, +5.30%): Successor-indexed BRAM replay cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.11%, with the final median Gowin Fmax of 78.390 MHz limiting throughput even after registering divisor-width checks at operand capture to shorten the divider's first-byte prefix path.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
