{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261120-003-r(3s2|5s2|6s2)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | jq -r '.id, .hypothesis.hypothesis'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r3s2\nRun a single speculative fetch stream with a two-entry circular\nfall-through queue. Store packed 64-bit words containing the full PC\nand instruction. Use one-bit read and write pointers and a two-bit\noccupancy count. Permit enqueue and dequeue on the same edge even\nwhen full. Drive decode and load-use detection from the head or, when\nempty, the live response. Advance the request PC to its predicted\nsuccessor only when imem is ready and the response is consumed\ndirectly or enqueued.\n\nUse the 64-entry direct-mapped successor table with the existing\nPC[7:2] index and full PC[31:8] tag. Store only the successor's low\n14 word-address bits. Reconstruct an aligned local prediction using\nthe request PC's upper 16 bits and use full-width PC+4 on a miss.\nTrain on advancing non-trapping control transfers, including\nconditional fall-throughs, and invalidate the indexed entry on a\ncross-page successor. The shorter payload reduces predictor LUT RAM.\n\nThe queue head PC, or the current request PC when the queue is empty,\nis exactly the successor predicted for the instruction in EX. Compare\nthis address with the full architectural successor on every valid EX\nadvance, including for stale entries on ordinary instructions.\nRegistered recovery discards all younger queue entries and\nannuls the younger ID/EX slot, clearing its valid bit even if a\ndata-bus hold prevents the rest of ID/EX from advancing. Fetch from\nthe registered correction address immediately. When imem accepts the\ncorrection, let ID consume the corrected response directly or enqueue\nit into entry zero if ID is held. Reset the queue pointers for the\nrecovered stream and keep the correction address selected across\nimem backpressure. A ready corrected instruction can enter ID on the\nrecovery cycle.\nPreserve full-width, word-aligned addresses, architectural trap\nhandling, and actual RVFI successor reporting.\n\nA temporary circular queue prototype completed marker-bracketed\nCoreMark in 4,008,388 cycles with both bus stalls and canonical CRCs,\ndown 5.02%\n…[6809 of 9881 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7b588edda93bccde]…\nes against\nthe registered decode PC independently of this recovery selection.\nThe direct path preserves the retirement interval when a redirect\nis followed by division. Qualify writes, data-bus requests,\nM-unit launches and training with effective EX validity. Preserve\nfull-width architectural targets, alignment traps, actual RVFI\nsuccessors and single-channel retirement.\n\nA fully registered scratch version failed liveness when a redirect\nled to division. The recovery bypass passed the same liveness check\nand both PC-continuity checks. The tagged scratch prototype with\nthat bypass passed stalled CoreMark validation in 3,994,772 marked\ncycles, down 7.39%. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 66.587,\n74.278 and 74.278 MHz. The median yields 185.94 iter/s, up 8.37%\nfrom 171.57. At that cycle count the 4.6% acceptance margin requires\na median above 71.691 MHz. Option 0 used 5,880 LUT4s and 144 LUT RAM cells.\nSelftest and all three random programs matched the ISS with zero\nwait states and with both buses stalled.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
